const STUDY_CONTENT = {
  "1": "<div class=\"hero\">\n  <div class=\"eyebrow\">Peters Family Homeschool · AP U.S. Government &amp; Politics</div>\n  <h1>Foundations of American Democracy</h1>\n  <p class=\"sub\">Every later unit in this course — Congress, the presidency, civil liberties, elections — is really just this unit's ideas showing up again in a new setting. The founders' arguments about power, factions, and federalism aren't historical trivia; they're the actual operating logic behind decisions the government makes this week. Get this unit solid, and the rest of the course reads like a sequel you already know the plot of.</p>\n  <div class=\"hero-pills\">\n    <span class=\"pill\">Exam weight 15–22%</span>\n    <span class=\"pill\">7 topics</span>\n    <span class=\"pill\">65 practice questions</span>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"toc\">\n  <div class=\"toc-label\">Jump to a section</div>\n  <ol>\n    <li><a href=\"#s11\">1.1 Ideals and Types of Democracy</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s12\">1.2 Natural Rights and the Declaration of Independence</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s13\">1.3 The Articles of Confederation and Their Failures</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s14\">1.4 Ratifying the Constitution — Federalists vs. Anti-Federalists</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s15\">1.5 Separation of Powers and Checks and Balances</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s16\">1.6 Federalism — Dividing Power Between National and State Governments</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s17\">1.7 Federalism in Practice</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#practice\">Practice Set</a></li>\n  </ol>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"wrap\">\n\n  <div class=\"skillbox\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label skill\">Master This First — Every Institution Answers \"Who Gets Power, and How Is It Checked?\"</span>\n    <p>This whole course keeps circling back to one tension: concentrated power is efficient but dangerous; distributed power is safer but slower and messier. Nearly every structure you'll learn about this year — separation of powers, federalism, the Bill of Rights, judicial review — is the founders' attempt to manage that exact tradeoff. Whenever a new institution or rule feels arbitrary, ask what concentration of power it's trying to prevent, or what problem of the Articles of Confederation it's specifically responding to. That question almost always unlocks the \"why,\" not just the \"what.\"</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s11\"><span class=\"num\">1.1</span>Ideals and Types of Democracy</h2>\n  <p>Before getting into American institutions specifically, it's worth being precise about what \"democracy\" even means — because the term covers several genuinely different models of how ordinary people's preferences are supposed to translate into government action.</p>\n\n  <table class=\"formula-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Model of Democracy</th><th>Core Idea</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Participatory</td><td>Broad citizen engagement (voting, activism, town halls) is what legitimizes government.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Pluralist</td><td>Competing organized groups (interest groups) negotiate and bargain to influence policy.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Elite</td><td>A small, well-resourced group of elites (economic, educated, or institutional) holds disproportionate influence over outcomes.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — These Models Aren't Mutually Exclusive</span>\n    <p>American democracy in practice shows evidence of all three models operating simultaneously, often around the exact same policy issue. A town hall meeting on a local zoning question is participatory democracy in action; the lobbying campaigns and advocacy coalitions fighting over that same issue are pluralist democracy; the fact that a handful of wealthy developers or long-tenured officials often have outsized sway over the outcome is elite theory in action. The AP exam frequently asks you to identify which model best explains a specific described scenario — the skill is matching the scenario's mechanism (mass participation? competing groups? a small powerful few?) to the right label, not picking a \"correct\" model in some abstract sense.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>Don't assume \"elite theory\" is a value judgment or an accusation of corruption — it's a descriptive model about where influence concentrates, not an editorial claim that elites are acting improperly. Similarly, don't assume pluralism means every interest group has equal power; pluralist theory specifically acknowledges some groups are better-resourced and more influential than others, while still describing a system of group-based competition rather than centralized elite control.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 1</span>\n    <p><strong>Identifying a Model of Democracy.</strong> A proposed environmental regulation faces intense lobbying from both an industry trade association and a coalition of environmental advocacy groups, each trying to shape the bill's final language. Which model of democracy does this scenario best illustrate?</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">The defining feature here is competing ORGANIZED GROUPS negotiating over policy outcomes — not mass citizen participation (that would be participatory) and not a single small elite dictating terms unilaterally (that would be elite theory). Two distinct, competing interest groups bargaining for influence over the same piece of legislation is a textbook description of <strong>pluralist democracy</strong>.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — The Gettysburg Address Reasserts Democratic Ideals</span>\n    <p>Lincoln's Gettysburg Address (1863) — one of this course's required foundational documents — was delivered during the Civil War, at a moment when the survival of the American constitutional experiment itself was in doubt. Its most famous line defines American government as \"government of the people, by the people, for the people\" — a compact statement of popular sovereignty: legitimate governing authority originates with ordinary citizens, not with a monarch, an elite class, or the state itself. Lincoln explicitly ties this back to the Declaration of Independence's founding claim that \"all men are created equal,\" reasserting that principle as the nation's core purpose at a moment when the Union's commitment to it was being tested by war. On the exam, treat the Gettysburg Address as reaffirming and applying the Declaration's equality and popular-sovereignty ideals, not as introducing a new philosophical argument of its own.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>Don't treat the Gettysburg Address as a legal or structural document — like the Declaration of Independence, it establishes no institutions and has no binding legal authority. Its role on the exam is rhetorical and philosophical: it restates and applies the democratic ideals of popular sovereignty and equality first articulated in the Declaration, rather than creating any new governmental mechanism. Also don't confuse \"government of the people, by the people, for the people\" with a specific model of democracy from the table above — it's a general statement of popular sovereignty underlying American democracy as a whole, not a description of participatory, pluralist, or elite theory specifically.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 1B</span>\n    <p><strong>Connecting the Gettysburg Address to Earlier Ideas.</strong> Explain how Lincoln's Gettysburg Address connects to the Declaration of Independence's natural rights philosophy.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">The Declaration of Independence claims that \"all men are created equal\" and that legitimate government rests on the consent of the governed. The Gettysburg Address, delivered nearly 90 years later during the Civil War, reasserts that exact equality principle and frames the war as a test of whether a nation \"conceived in Liberty, and dedicated to the proposition that all men are created equal\" can endure. Lincoln's phrase \"government of the people, by the people, for the people\" restates the Declaration's consent-of-the-governed idea as a definition of popular sovereignty — both documents ground legitimate government in the equal standing and authority of ordinary people, rather than in any inherited or elite claim to power.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Distinguish participatory, pluralist, and elite models of democracy, and identify which best explains a given scenario.</li>\n      <li>Explain why these three models can coexist within the same political system rather than being mutually exclusive.</li>\n      <li>Avoid treating \"elite theory\" as an inherently negative or corrupt characterization.</li>\n      <li>Explain how the Gettysburg Address reasserts the Declaration of Independence's equality principle through the idea of popular sovereignty (\"of the people, by the people, for the people\").</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s12\"><span class=\"num\">1.2</span>Natural Rights and the Declaration of Independence</h2>\n  <p>The Declaration of Independence — one of this course's 9 required foundational documents — lays out the philosophical justification for the entire American project, built on ideas of natural rights and the social contract that trace back to Enlightenment thinkers like John Locke.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Natural Rights Exist Independent of Government</span>\n    <p>The Declaration's core philosophical claim is that certain rights — \"Life, Liberty and the pursuit of Happiness\" — belong to individuals simply by virtue of being human, not because a government grants them. Government's job, in this framing, is to SECURE those pre-existing rights, not create them. This is exactly why the Declaration argues that when a government becomes destructive of these rights, the people have the authority to \"alter or abolish it\" — legitimate government exists by the consent of the governed, and that consent can be withdrawn.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>The Declaration of Independence is a statement of political philosophy and grievances against King George III — it is NOT the founding legal document of the U.S. government and has no binding legal authority today. Don't confuse it with the Constitution, which is the actual legal framework establishing the government's structure and powers. A common exam trap is attributing structural features of government (like separation of powers) to the Declaration when they actually come from the Constitution.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 2</span>\n    <p><strong>Applying Social Contract Theory.</strong> Explain how the Declaration of Independence uses the concept of the social contract to justify separation from Britain.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">Social contract theory holds that government's legitimate authority comes from an implicit agreement with the governed: people consent to be governed in exchange for the government protecting their natural rights. The Declaration argues that King George III violated this contract — it lists specific grievances (taxation without representation, quartering troops, dissolving colonial legislatures) as evidence the government failed to secure the colonists' natural rights. Since the government broke its side of the agreement, the Declaration argues the colonists were justified in withdrawing their consent and forming a new government — the social contract had effectively been voided by the Crown's own actions.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Explain the concept of natural rights and how it differs from rights granted by government.</li>\n      <li>Explain how social contract theory justifies both the formation and the dissolution of a government.</li>\n      <li>Distinguish the Declaration of Independence's philosophical role from the Constitution's legal role.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Government Power Exists in Tension with Individual Liberty</span>\n    <p>Enough government power is needed to provide order, security, and the \"general welfare\" — but too much power threatens the very liberty government is supposed to protect. The Constitution's Preamble captures this balance directly, listing goals like \"insure domestic Tranquility\" and \"provide for the common defence\" alongside \"secure the Blessings of Liberty.\" Nearly every structural choice covered in this unit — separation of powers, federalism, a written Bill of Rights — is an attempt to grant government enough power to function while still constraining it enough to protect individual rights.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s13\"><span class=\"num\">1.3</span>The Articles of Confederation and Their Failures</h2>\n  <p>The Articles of Confederation — the second required foundational document — was the nation's first attempt at a governing framework, ratified in 1781. It's genuinely important to study not because it succeeded, but because its specific failures directly explain nearly every design choice made in the Constitution that replaced it.</p>\n\n  <table class=\"formula-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Weakness of the Articles</th><th>Constitutional Fix</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>No power to tax; government depended on voluntary state contributions</td><td>Congress given the power to levy taxes directly</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>No national executive to enforce laws</td><td>Creation of the presidency</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>No national judiciary to resolve disputes</td><td>Creation of the federal court system</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>No power to regulate interstate commerce</td><td>Congress given the Commerce Clause power</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Amendment required unanimous consent of all states</td><td>Amendment process eased to proposal by 2/3 of both houses of Congress (or a convention called by 2/3 of state legislatures), then ratification by 3/4 of the states</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Each state had just one vote, regardless of population</td><td>Bicameral Congress, one house based on population</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Shays' Rebellion Was the Breaking Point</span>\n    <p>Article V's two-stage process — a congressional (or convention) supermajority to <em>propose</em> an amendment, followed by ratification by three-fourths of the states — is still deliberately demanding, but far more workable than the Articles' requirement that every single state agree. A common exam trap is remembering only the \"3/4 of states\" ratification threshold and forgetting the congressional supermajority needed just to propose an amendment in the first place.</p>\n\n  <p>Shays' Rebellion (1786–87), an armed uprising of indebted Massachusetts farmers, exposed just how powerless the national government under the Articles really was — it had no standing army and no reliable way to fund a response, and the rebellion had to be put down by a privately funded state militia instead. This crisis is widely credited with convincing skeptical delegates that a genuinely stronger national government was necessary, directly setting the stage for the Constitutional Convention in 1787.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>The Articles of Confederation didn't fail because it gave too much power to the national government — it's almost the exact opposite. The Articles created a deliberately WEAK national government (a direct reaction to fears of a tyrannical monarchy like the one just overthrown) that ended up too weak to function. Don't describe the Articles' failure as \"too much centralized power\" — the defining problem was the reverse.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 3</span>\n    <p><strong>Connecting a Weakness to Its Fix.</strong> Explain how the Articles of Confederation's lack of federal taxing power created a specific practical problem, and how the Constitution addressed it.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">Under the Articles, the national government could only REQUEST funds from the states — it had no enforcement mechanism, and states routinely fell short or ignored requests entirely. This left Congress chronically unable to pay Revolutionary War debts or fund basic operations, undermining the new nation's credibility and stability. The Constitution directly addressed this by granting Congress the enumerated power to \"lay and collect Taxes\" directly from citizens and businesses (Article I, Section 8), giving the federal government an independent and enforceable revenue source for the first time.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Identify the key structural weaknesses of the Articles of Confederation.</li>\n      <li>Explain how Shays' Rebellion exposed those weaknesses and prompted constitutional reform.</li>\n      <li>Match each weakness of the Articles to the specific constitutional provision designed to fix it.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s14\"><span class=\"num\">1.4</span>Ratifying the Constitution — Federalists vs. Anti-Federalists</h2>\n  <p>Getting the new Constitution ratified required winning a genuine public argument, fought out largely in newspapers and pamphlets — and two of this course's required foundational documents, Federalist No. 10 and Brutus No. 1, come directly from that debate.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — The Central Disagreement Was About the Size and Scope of the Republic</span>\n    <p>Anti-Federalists, writing under pen names like \"Brutus,\" argued that a republic needed to stay small and geographically compact to remain accountable to the people — a government spanning a vast, diverse country would inevitably grow distant and tyrannical, and they specifically feared a strong national government would swallow up individual liberty and state authority. Federalists like James Madison (writing as \"Publius\" in Federalist No. 10) argued the exact opposite: a LARGE republic was actually safer, because it would contain so many competing factions and interests that no single faction could easily dominate the whole government — an extended republic diluted rather than concentrated the danger of majority tyranny.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>Federalist No. 10's argument about factions is one of the single most frequently tested ideas in this entire course, and it's easy to state backward under pressure. Madison does NOT argue that factions should be eliminated — he explicitly says eliminating factions would require destroying liberty itself (since factions arise naturally whenever people are free to hold different opinions and interests). Instead, his solution is to CONTROL the EFFECTS of factions by expanding the republic, making it structurally difficult for any single faction to gain a dominating majority.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 4</span>\n    <p><strong>Applying Federalist No. 10.</strong> A small town government is dominated entirely by one local industry's interests, which consistently override the concerns of every other group in town. Using Madison's argument in Federalist No. 10, explain why this outcome would be less likely at the national level.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">Madison's core claim is that a larger, more diverse republic contains a greater number and variety of competing factions and interests. In a small town dominated by a single industry, that one faction can easily form a majority, since there simply aren't many other competing interests to check it. Extend the same scenario to the scale of an entire nation, and that single industry now has to compete against a much wider range of other economic interests, regional concerns, and social groups — making it far harder for any one faction, including that industry, to assemble a dominating national majority. This is exactly Madison's argument for why an extended republic controls the EFFECTS of factions, even without eliminating factions themselves.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Article VII's Ratification Threshold</span>\n    <p>Under <strong>Article VII</strong>, the Constitution would take effect (among the ratifying states) once ratified by special conventions in <strong>9 of the 13 states</strong> — a deliberate departure from the Articles' unanimity requirement. Notably, ratification was entrusted to specially elected ratifying conventions in each state, not a vote of the sitting state legislatures, precisely because the Constitution's supporters wanted a more direct expression of popular consent than routing the decision through existing state political institutions.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Explain the core disagreement between Federalists and Anti-Federalists regarding the size and scope of the republic.</li>\n      <li>State Madison's argument in Federalist No. 10 accurately: control the effects of factions, don't eliminate their causes.</li>\n      <li>Apply Federalist No. 10's logic to a new scenario involving factional influence.</li>\n      <li>State the ratification threshold set by Article VII: conventions in 9 of the 13 states, not a vote of state legislatures.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s15\"><span class=\"num\">1.5</span>Separation of Powers and Checks and Balances</h2>\n  <p>Federalist No. 51 — another required document, also by Madison — lays out the structural mechanism the Constitution uses to prevent any single branch of government from accumulating too much power: dividing that power among three branches, then giving each branch tools to check the others.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — \"Ambition Must Be Made to Counteract Ambition\"</span>\n    <p>Madison's famous phrase captures the entire logic of checks and balances: rather than relying on good intentions or virtue to restrain government officials, the Constitution's design assumes officials WILL be ambitious and self-interested, and builds a system where each branch's own self-interest motivates it to check the others. Congress can override a presidential veto; the president nominates judges but the Senate must confirm them; courts can rule laws unconstitutional. No branch fully trusts the others to police themselves — instead, competing institutional self-interest does the policing.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <table class=\"formula-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>A Key Check It Holds Over Another Branch</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Legislative (Congress)</td><td>Can override a presidential veto with a 2/3 vote in both chambers; can impeach and remove the president or federal judges</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Executive (President)</td><td>Can veto legislation; nominates federal judges and executive officials</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Judicial (Courts)</td><td>Can rule laws or executive actions unconstitutional (judicial review)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>Separation of powers and checks and balances are related but distinct concepts, and mixing them up costs easy points. Separation of powers is the basic STRUCTURAL division of government into three branches, each with its own primary function (legislative, executive, judicial). Checks and balances describes the specific TOOLS each branch has to limit the others' power. A question asking you to \"identify a check\" wants a specific tool (like the veto or judicial review) — not a restatement that there are three branches.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 5</span>\n    <p><strong>Identifying a Specific Check.</strong> The Senate votes down a president's nominee for a federal judgeship. Identify which branch is checking which, and explain the constitutional basis.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">This is the LEGISLATIVE branch (specifically the Senate) checking the EXECUTIVE branch's nomination power. The Constitution (Article II, Section 2) requires that presidential nominations for federal judgeships receive the Senate's \"advice and consent\" — meaning the president cannot unilaterally place judges on the federal bench. By voting down the nominee, the Senate is exercising exactly the check this clause was designed to provide, preventing the president's appointment power from going unchecked.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Explain Madison's reasoning in Federalist No. 51 for why checks and balances are necessary.</li>\n      <li>Distinguish separation of powers (structural division) from checks and balances (specific tools).</li>\n      <li>Identify a specific constitutional check, given a described scenario, and explain which branch is checking which.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s16\"><span class=\"num\">1.6</span>Federalism — Dividing Power Between National and State Governments</h2>\n  <p>Federalism is the division of governing authority between a national government and state governments, each with genuine, independent authority over certain matters — a deliberate middle ground between the too-weak confederation of the Articles and a fully centralized unitary government.</p>\n\n  <table class=\"formula-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type of Power</th><th>Description</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Enumerated (expressed) powers</td><td>Powers explicitly granted to the national government in the Constitution</td><td>Coining money, declaring war, regulating interstate commerce</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Reserved powers</td><td>Powers not granted to the national government, reserved to the states (10th Amendment)</td><td>Establishing schools, marriage laws, most policing power</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Concurrent powers</td><td>Powers held by both national and state governments simultaneously</td><td>Taxation, building roads, establishing courts</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Implied powers</td><td>Powers not explicitly listed in the Constitution, but inferred as necessary to carry out an enumerated power (Necessary and Proper Clause)</td><td>Chartering a national bank (McCulloch v. Maryland)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — The Necessary and Proper Clause Expands National Power</span>\n    <p>Article I, Section 8's \"Necessary and Proper Clause\" (sometimes called the \"Elastic Clause\") gives Congress authority to pass laws needed to carry out its enumerated powers — and this clause has historically been interpreted quite broadly, expanding federal power well beyond the literal, narrow list of enumerated powers. This clause was directly at issue in <strong>McCulloch v. Maryland (1819)</strong>, one of this course's required cases: the Supreme Court ruled that Congress had implied authority to charter a national bank (not explicitly listed as an enumerated power) because doing so was \"necessary and proper\" for carrying out other enumerated powers like taxation and regulating commerce. The same case also established that states cannot tax federal institutions, reinforcing the principle of national supremacy over conflicting state law.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — The Anti-Commandeering Doctrine Limits the Supremacy Clause</span>\n    <p>One important limit on the Supremacy Clause: while valid federal law overrides conflicting state law, the federal government generally cannot force state governments or officials to use their own resources to enforce federal law — a principle known as the <strong>anti-commandeering doctrine</strong> (established in cases like <em>New York v. United States</em> and <em>Printz v. United States</em>, both non-required but useful context). This means a state can decline to actively help enforce a federal law it disagrees with, even though the underlying federal law remains legally supreme and federal officials can still enforce it themselves.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>McCulloch v. Maryland is genuinely a two-part holding, and exam questions often test only one half, or conflate the two: (1) Congress has IMPLIED powers via the Necessary and Proper Clause, beyond its literal enumerated list, and (2) the Supremacy Clause means valid federal law overrides conflicting state law (so Maryland couldn't tax the federally-chartered bank). Make sure you can state both halves separately, since a question might ask specifically about implied powers, specifically about federal supremacy, or about how the two connect.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 6</span>\n    <p><strong>Applying McCulloch v. Maryland.</strong> Congress passes a law creating a new federal regulatory agency, an action not explicitly listed among Congress's enumerated powers. A state attempts to block the agency's operations within its borders. Using the precedent of McCulloch v. Maryland, predict how a court would likely rule.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">Following McCulloch's precedent, a court would likely find TWO things: first, that Congress has implied authority under the Necessary and Proper Clause to create the agency, as long as it's reasonably connected to carrying out an enumerated power (just as chartering a bank was \"necessary and proper\" for taxation and commerce powers). Second, following the Supremacy Clause, the state would NOT be permitted to block a validly-created federal agency's operations — state law cannot override or obstruct legitimate federal authority, the same principle that prevented Maryland from taxing the national bank.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Federalist No. 39 Defends the Constitution's Mixed National/Federal Character</span>\n    <p>Federalist No. 39 — another required document, also by Madison — directly addresses a core structural question: is the proposed Constitution creating a national government or a federal (confederal) one? Madison's answer is that it's genuinely both: the Constitution, he writes, is \"neither a national nor a federal Constitution, but a composition of both.\" Some features are federal — the Constitution was ratified state-by-state by the people acting in their separate state capacities, and the states retain independent authority over reserved powers. Other features are national — the national government operates directly on individual citizens (not just on states as units, the way a true confederation would), and federal law is supreme over conflicting state law. This \"compound\" or hybrid structure is the direct textual basis for the enumerated/reserved/concurrent/implied powers framework covered above: it exists precisely because the Constitution deliberately blends federal and national elements rather than choosing one model cleanly.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>Don't confuse Federalist No. 39 with Federalist No. 10 or No. 51 — all three are Madison, but they answer different questions. No. 10 defends the LARGE REPUBLIC as a way to control the effects of factions. No. 51 defends SEPARATION OF POWERS AND CHECKS AND BALANCES among the three branches, so that \"ambition counteracts ambition.\" No. 39 defends FEDERALISM ITSELF — the division and blending of authority between national and state governments — arguing the Constitution is a hybrid \"partly national, partly federal\" system rather than a purely national or purely confederal one. If a question is about factions, it's No. 10; about branches checking each other, it's No. 51; about the national-state power balance, it's No. 39.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 6B</span>\n    <p><strong>Applying Federalist No. 39.</strong> A critic argues the proposed Constitution creates a fully national government that erases the states' independent authority. Using Madison's argument in Federalist No. 39, explain why this criticism is inaccurate.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">Madison's Federalist No. 39 argues the Constitution is a hybrid, not a purely national government. Federal features include the national government acting directly on individuals and federal law's supremacy over conflicting state law — real features supporting the critic's concern. But federal (confederal) features remain too: the Constitution was ratified by the people acting through their separate states, not by a single national vote, and the states retain independent, reserved authority over matters the Constitution doesn't grant to the national government. Because both national and federal characteristics coexist by design, Madison's argument directly rebuts the claim that the Constitution fully erases state authority — it deliberately preserves a meaningful, independent role for the states alongside a stronger national government.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Distinguish enumerated, reserved, concurrent, and <strong>implied</strong> powers, with an example of each.</li>\n      <li>State both halves of the McCulloch v. Maryland holding: implied powers and federal supremacy.</li>\n      <li>Apply McCulloch's precedent to a new scenario involving a conflict between federal and state authority.</li>\n      <li>Explain Federalist No. 39's \"partly national, partly federal\" argument, and distinguish it from Federalist No. 10 (factions) and No. 51 (checks and balances).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s17\"><span class=\"num\">1.7</span>Federalism in Practice</h2>\n  <p>Federalism isn't a fixed, unchanging balance — the actual relationship between national and state power has shifted substantially over American history, and the Constitution provides several concrete tools that shape how that relationship plays out in practice.</p>\n\n  <table class=\"formula-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>How It Shapes Federalism</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Categorical grants</td><td>Federal funding to states for a narrowly defined purpose, with specific conditions attached</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Block grants</td><td>Federal funding to states for a broader purpose, with more state discretion over how it's spent</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Mandates</td><td>Federal requirements states must follow, sometimes without accompanying funding (\"unfunded mandates\")</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Grants Are a Way to Influence States Without Directly Commanding Them</span>\n    <p>Since the Constitution reserves many policy areas to the states, the federal government often can't simply order states to adopt a specific policy directly. Categorical grants are a workaround: the federal government offers funding, conditioned on the state adopting certain rules. States technically remain free to refuse the money and the conditions — but in practice, the financial incentive is often powerful enough to function almost like a command, a dynamic sometimes called \"fiscal federalism.\" A classic example: federal highway funding conditioned on states raising their legal drinking age, used to nudge state policy in a Constitutionally-reserved area without directly regulating it.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>Don't assume federalism has moved in only one direction throughout American history. While the overall long-run trend has generally been toward greater national power (especially since the New Deal era and cases like the broad reading of the Commerce Clause), the pendulum has also swung back toward states at points — <strong>United States v. Lopez (1995)</strong>, a required case, is a key example of the Supreme Court actually LIMITING federal Commerce Clause power, ruling that Congress had overstepped by banning guns near schools under the Commerce Clause, since gun possession near a school isn't substantially related to interstate commerce. Federalism's balance is genuinely dynamic, not a one-way ratchet toward Washington. This shift of power and policy responsibility back toward the states — whether through Supreme Court rulings like Lopez, block grants that increase state discretion, or deliberate policy choices — is called <strong>devolution</strong>, and it's the opposite of the centralizing trend of the New Deal era.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 7</span>\n    <p><strong>Categorical Grant vs. Mandate.</strong> Explain the key difference between a categorical grant and an unfunded mandate, using a hypothetical education policy example for each.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">A categorical grant would involve the federal government OFFERING states money for a specific education purpose — for instance, funding for reading programs, conditioned on the state implementing certain curriculum standards. States can decline the money (and the conditions) if they choose. An unfunded mandate, by contrast, would REQUIRE states to implement a specific education policy — say, a testing requirement — without providing federal funding to cover the cost of compliance. The key distinction is financial: a grant is an incentive states can refuse; a mandate is often an obligation states must meet regardless of whether funding accompanies it, which is exactly why unfunded mandates are frequently controversial among state governments.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Distinguish categorical grants, block grants, and mandates as tools of federalism.</li>\n      <li>Explain how fiscal federalism lets the national government influence state policy in constitutionally reserved areas.</li>\n      <li>Explain United States v. Lopez as an example of the Supreme Court limiting federal power, not just expanding it.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"practice\">Unit 1 Practice Set</h2>\n  <p>Work through every problem before checking the answer key — for anything asking you to \"explain,\" write out the actual reasoning in full sentences, exactly like the real exam expects.</p>\n\n  <h3>Conceptual</h3>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">1</span><span>A neighborhood association votes directly on whether to build a new park, with every resident able to cast a vote. Which model of democracy does this best illustrate?</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">2</span><span>Explain, in one or two sentences, why the Declaration of Independence is not a legally binding document today.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">3</span><span>Identify two specific weaknesses of the Articles of Confederation and the constitutional provision that addressed each.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">4</span><span>Explain Madison's argument in Federalist No. 10 for why a large, diverse republic is safer than a small one.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">5</span><span>A new federal law requires states to implement a specific pollution-monitoring program but provides no funding to do so. What tool of federalism does this describe?</span></div>\n\n  <h3>Application</h3>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">6</span><span>The president vetoes a bill passed by Congress, and Congress subsequently overrides the veto with a 2/3 vote in both chambers. Identify the two checks being exercised, in order.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">7</span><span>A state passes a law directly conflicting with a valid federal statute. Using the Supremacy Clause (as reinforced in McCulloch v. Maryland), predict the likely outcome.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">8</span><span>Congress passes a law using its power under the Commerce Clause to regulate an activity with only a very weak connection to interstate commerce. Using United States v. Lopez as precedent, evaluate whether this law would likely survive a court challenge.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">9</span><span>A state receives federal funds for road construction, with broad discretion over exactly how the money is spent within that general purpose. Identify this type of grant.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">10</span><span>An Anti-Federalist writer argues that a proposed national government would be too distant from ordinary citizens to remain accountable. Which required foundational document does this argument most likely come from?</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">11</span><span>Explain why Shays' Rebellion is considered a turning point in building support for a stronger national government.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">12</span><span>Identify whether \"establishing local school curricula\" is an example of an enumerated, reserved, or concurrent power, and explain why.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">13</span><span>A president appoints a new cabinet secretary; the Senate must vote to confirm the appointment. Which principle from Federalist No. 51 does this illustrate?</span></div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"margin-top:30px;\">Answer Key</h3>\n  <div class=\"answer-key\">\n    <details><summary>Question 1</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Participatory democracy — this scenario is defined by broad, direct citizen engagement in a decision, rather than competing organized groups (pluralist) or a small dominant elite (elite theory).</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 2</summary><div class=\"a-content\">The Declaration of Independence is a statement of political philosophy and a list of grievances justifying separation from Britain — it never established a legal governing structure. The Constitution, not the Declaration, is the actual binding legal framework for U.S. government.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 3</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Example: no power to tax (fixed by Congress's constitutional taxing power) and no national executive (fixed by creation of the presidency). Other valid pairs include: no national judiciary (fixed by the federal court system), or unanimous amendment requirement (fixed by the 3/4-states amendment process).</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 4</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Madison argues that a large republic contains a greater number and variety of competing factions, making it structurally difficult for any single faction to gain a dominating majority — this controls the harmful EFFECTS of factions without needing to eliminate their causes, which Madison says would require destroying liberty itself.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 5</summary><div class=\"a-content\">An unfunded mandate — a federal requirement imposed on states without accompanying federal funding to cover compliance costs.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 6</summary><div class=\"a-content\">First, the executive branch checks the legislative branch via the presidential veto. Second, the legislative branch checks the executive branch back by overriding that veto with the required 2/3 supermajority in both chambers.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 7</summary><div class=\"a-content\">The federal statute would prevail. Under the Supremacy Clause, as reinforced in McCulloch v. Maryland, valid federal law overrides conflicting state law — states cannot obstruct or nullify legitimate federal authority.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 8</summary><div class=\"a-content\">The law would likely be struck down. United States v. Lopez established that Congress's Commerce Clause power does not extend to activities with only a weak or indirect connection to interstate commerce — the Court limited federal power in this area rather than deferring to Congress's judgment.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 9</summary><div class=\"a-content\">A block grant — funding for a broad purpose with significant state discretion over the specifics of implementation, as opposed to a categorical grant's narrower, more tightly conditioned funding.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 10</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Brutus No. 1 — the Anti-Federalist argument that a large, extended republic would become too distant and unaccountable to ordinary citizens, in direct opposition to Madison's Federalist No. 10 argument.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 11</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Shays' Rebellion exposed that the national government under the Articles of Confederation had no standing army and no reliable way to fund a response to internal unrest, demonstrating the practical dangers of a national government too weak to maintain order — this crisis helped convince skeptics that stronger national authority was necessary, leading directly to the Constitutional Convention.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 12</summary><div class=\"a-content\">A reserved power. Education is not listed among the national government's enumerated powers in the Constitution, so under the 10th Amendment, authority over it is reserved to the states.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 13</summary><div class=\"a-content\">This illustrates \"ambition must be made to counteract ambition\" — the Senate's confirmation power checks the president's appointment power, exactly the kind of institutional self-interest-driven check Madison describes in Federalist No. 51.</div></details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"footer-nav\">\n    Next up: head to the <strong>Unit 1 QBank</strong> to drill these ideas, then move on to <strong>Unit 2 — Interactions Among Branches of Government</strong>.\n  </div>\n\n</div>\n",
  "2": "<div class=\"hero\">\n  <div class=\"eyebrow\">Peters Family Homeschool · AP U.S. Government &amp; Politics</div>\n  <h1>Interactions Among Branches of Government</h1>\n  <p class=\"sub\">This is the biggest unit on the exam, and for good reason — it's where Unit 1's abstract ideas about separation of powers and checks and balances turn into the actual, specific machinery of Congress, the presidency, the courts, and the bureaucracy. Every institution here exists in constant tension with the other three, exactly as the founders designed.</p>\n  <div class=\"hero-pills\">\n    <span class=\"pill\">Exam weight 25–36%</span>\n    <span class=\"pill\">8 topics</span>\n    <span class=\"pill\">65 practice questions</span>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"toc\">\n  <div class=\"toc-label\">Jump to a section</div>\n  <ol>\n    <li><a href=\"#s21\">2.1 Congress: Structure and Powers</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s22\">2.2 Congressional Behavior and Representation</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s23\">2.3 The Presidency: Powers and Growth</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s24\">2.4 Checks on Presidential Power</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s25\">2.5 The Judicial Branch: Structure and Judicial Review</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s26\">2.6 Judicial Decision-Making and Legitimacy</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s27\">2.7 The Federal Bureaucracy</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s28\">2.8 Policy-Making Across the Branches</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#practice\">Practice Set</a></li>\n  </ol>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"wrap\">\n\n  <div class=\"skillbox\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label skill\">Master This First — Formal Power vs. Informal Power</span>\n    <p>For every institution in this unit, keep two separate mental columns: what the Constitution EXPLICITLY grants (formal power), and what the institution has come to exercise in PRACTICE, often through custom, precedent, or creative interpretation (informal power). A president's veto is formal power, spelled out in Article II. A president's use of the \"bully pulpit\" to shape public opinion is informal power — nowhere written into the Constitution, but a real and significant tool nonetheless. This distinction shows up constantly across Congress, the presidency, and the courts, and exam questions frequently hinge on correctly sorting a described power into the right column.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s21\"><span class=\"num\">2.1</span>Congress: Structure and Powers</h2>\n  <p>Congress is bicameral — split into the House of Representatives and the Senate — and that split isn't a redundancy. The two chambers were deliberately designed with different structures, different constituencies, and different institutional personalities.</p>\n\n  <table class=\"formula-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>House of Representatives</th><th>Senate</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Membership</td><td>435 total, apportioned by state population</td><td>100 total, 2 per state regardless of population</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Term length</td><td>2 years</td><td>6 years, staggered (about 1/3 up for election every 2 years)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Constituency</td><td>Smaller, more local districts</td><td>Entire state</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Distinctive power</td><td>Revenue (tax) bills must originate here</td><td>Confirms nominations; ratifies treaties (2/3 vote)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Floor debate style</td><td>Tightly controlled by the Rules Committee; limited debate</td><td>Extended debate allowed; filibuster possible</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — The House Is Majoritarian; the Senate Protects the Minority</span>\n    <p>The House's rules (especially the powerful Rules Committee, which controls how and whether a bill reaches the floor) are built for speed and majority efficiency — a determined majority can generally move legislation through relatively quickly. The Senate's rules do the opposite: unlimited debate historically allowed the filibuster (a tactic to delay or block a vote through extended debate), which can only be shut down by invoking cloture, requiring a 60-vote supermajority in most cases. This means a simple 51-vote Senate majority often isn't enough to actually pass legislation — a genuinely important distinction that trips up a lot of students who assume \"majority\" always means \"enough votes.\"</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>Don't confuse a chamber's total membership with what's needed to pass ordinary legislation. Passing a typical bill in either chamber requires only a simple majority of those present and voting — it's the SENATE'S FILIBUSTER RULE specifically (via the 60-vote cloture threshold) that can effectively raise the real bar for controversial legislation, not a constitutional supermajority requirement built into lawmaking generally. Treaties (2/3 Senate vote) and veto overrides (2/3 in both chambers) are the genuine constitutional supermajority requirements — the filibuster is a Senate procedural rule, not a constitutional one, and it can be changed by the Senate itself. One important exception: bills passed through <strong>budget reconciliation</strong> (a special process for budget-related legislation under the Congressional Budget Act) are not subject to the filibuster and can pass the Senate with a simple majority — which is why major tax and spending bills sometimes pass with far fewer than 60 votes even though ordinary legislation cannot.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 1</span>\n    <p><strong>Identifying the Right Chamber.</strong> A senator wants to block a bill they oppose, which currently has 55 votes in favor — a majority, but short of 60. Explain what tool the senator can use, and why it works.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">The senator can use (or threaten) a <strong>filibuster</strong> — extending debate indefinitely to prevent a final vote from ever occurring. Because ending debate requires invoking cloture, which needs 60 votes in most cases, a bill with only 55 supporters can be blocked even though it technically has majority support. This is exactly the structural feature that makes the Senate function differently from the House, where the Rules Committee can generally force a vote without needing anything close to 60 votes.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Committee Types, Seniority, and How a Bill Actually Becomes a Law</span>\n    <p>Most of Congress's substantive work happens in committee, and there are several distinct committee types: <strong>standing committees</strong> (permanent, subject-specific committees that do most of the substantive bill work — drafting, holding hearings, and \"marking up\" legislation), <strong>select/special committees</strong> (temporary committees, often investigative), <strong>joint committees</strong> (made up of members from both chambers), and <strong>conference committees</strong> (temporary committees that reconcile differing House and Senate versions of the same bill before final passage). Committee chairs are typically determined by the <strong>seniority system</strong> — usually the majority party's longest continuously serving member on that committee — which gives incumbency added institutional value beyond simply winning re-election. Putting it together, a bill's typical path runs: introduction → committee referral and markup → floor debate and amendment → passage in each chamber → a conference committee to reconcile any differences between the House and Senate versions → presentation to the president for signature or veto.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Compare the House and Senate on membership, term length, and distinctive constitutional powers.</li>\n      <li>Explain how the filibuster and cloture function, and why they make the Senate less purely majoritarian than the House.</li>\n      <li>Distinguish a constitutional supermajority requirement (treaties, veto overrides) from a Senate procedural rule (the filibuster).</li>\n      <li>Describe the standing/select/joint/conference committee typology, the seniority system, and the full path a bill follows to become law.</li>\n      <li>Explain why budget reconciliation bills are exempt from the filibuster and can pass with a simple majority.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s22\"><span class=\"num\">2.2</span>Congressional Behavior and Representation</h2>\n  <p>Members of Congress constantly navigate a tension between representing their constituents' immediate wishes and exercising their own independent judgment — a tension political scientists describe using a few standard models of representation.</p>\n\n  <table class=\"formula-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Trustee</td><td>The representative uses their own judgment and expertise, even against constituents' stated wishes.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Delegate</td><td>The representative directly follows constituents' expressed preferences, even against their own judgment.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Politico</td><td>A blend: acting as a delegate on some issues, a trustee on others, depending on context.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Redistricting Shapes Who Gets Represented, and How</span>\n    <p>Every ten years, following the census, states redraw congressional district lines to reflect population changes — and how those lines get drawn has enormous political consequences. <strong>Baker v. Carr (1962)</strong>, a required case, established that federal courts COULD hear redistricting cases at all (previously treated as a \"political question\" outside judicial reach), and set the \"one person, one vote\" principle requiring districts to have roughly equal populations. <strong>Shaw v. Reno (1993)</strong>, another required case, addressed racial gerrymandering specifically — ruling that districts drawn with race as the PREDOMINANT factor are subject to strict scrutiny and may violate the Equal Protection Clause, even when the stated goal is increasing minority representation.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>Baker v. Carr and Shaw v. Reno address two DIFFERENT redistricting problems, and conflating them is a common exam error. Baker v. Carr is about POPULATION EQUALITY between districts (making courts able to intervene when districts have wildly unequal populations). Shaw v. Reno is about RACIAL gerrymandering specifically (limiting how much race can drive district boundaries). A district could satisfy Baker v. Carr's population-equality standard while still violating Shaw v. Reno's limits on race-based drawing, or vice versa — they're addressing genuinely separate constitutional concerns.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Why Incumbents Usually Win</span>\n    <p>Sitting members of Congress enjoy substantial structural advantages in reelection: greater name recognition, easier fundraising (donors prefer proven winners with committee influence), free constituent-communication tools (the <strong>franking privilege</strong>), a track record of <strong>casework</strong> (helping constituents navigate federal agencies), and — often — a district drawn to their party's advantage. Members also build support through <strong>pork barrel spending</strong> (securing narrowly targeted federal funding for projects in their own district) and <strong>logrolling</strong> (trading votes — \"I'll support your project if you support mine\") to accumulate credit-claiming opportunities back home. Together these factors help explain the persistently high reelection rate for incumbents, even in years when Congress as an institution is broadly unpopular.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 2</span>\n    <p><strong>Applying a Model of Representation.</strong> A member of Congress personally supports a policy but votes against it after receiving overwhelming, consistent feedback from constituents opposing it. Which model of representation does this illustrate?</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">This is the <strong>delegate</strong> model — the representative is prioritizing constituents' expressed wishes over their own independent judgment or personal policy preference. If the representative had instead voted their own conscience despite constituent opposition, that would illustrate the trustee model instead.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Distinguish the trustee, delegate, and politico models of representation, and identify which applies to a given scenario.</li>\n      <li>Explain what Baker v. Carr established regarding redistricting and the \"one person, one vote\" principle.</li>\n      <li>Explain what Shaw v. Reno established regarding racial gerrymandering, and distinguish it from Baker v. Carr's population-equality concern.</li>\n      <li>Explain the incumbency advantage and how pork barrel spending and logrolling help sustain it.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s23\"><span class=\"num\">2.3</span>The Presidency: Powers and Growth</h2>\n  <p>Article II of the Constitution grants the president a comparatively short, somewhat vague list of formal powers — but the modern presidency has grown to exercise substantial influence well beyond that original text, largely through informal powers developed over time.</p>\n\n  <table class=\"formula-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Formal powers (explicit in Article II)</td><td>Commander in chief; veto legislation; appoint officials and judges (with Senate consent); make treaties (with Senate consent); grant pardons; receive ambassadors</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Informal powers (developed through practice)</td><td>Executive orders; executive agreements; signing statements; \"going public\" to mobilize public opinion; the \"bully pulpit\"</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Hamilton's Case for \"Energy in the Executive\"</span>\n    <p>Federalist No. 70, a required document, is Alexander Hamilton's argument for why the presidency should be held by a SINGLE person rather than a committee or council. Hamilton argues that a single executive provides \"energy\" — meaning decisiveness, speed, and the ability to act quickly, especially during a crisis — along with clearer accountability, since a single person can't hide behind shared blame the way a committee could. This argument directly shaped the choice for a unitary executive branch rather than the plural executive some founders had proposed.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Executive Privilege Is an Informal, Limited Power</span>\n    <p>Presidents also sometimes assert <strong>executive privilege</strong> — an informal, constitutionally unwritten claim that certain executive branch communications (especially with close advisers) can be withheld from Congress or the courts. Like other informal powers, it is not absolute: in <strong>United States v. Nixon (1974)</strong>, the Supreme Court ruled that executive privilege must yield to a specific, demonstrated need for evidence in a criminal proceeding.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>Executive orders and executive agreements are powerful tools, but they are informal powers, not explicitly granted by the Constitution's text — and they're also more fragile than formally-enacted law. An executive order can be reversed by a subsequent president simply issuing a new one, and it can be challenged and struck down by courts if it exceeds the president's authority or conflicts with existing statute. Don't treat executive orders as having the same permanence or legal weight as a law passed by Congress and signed into effect.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 3</span>\n    <p><strong>Formal vs. Informal Power.</strong> A president issues an executive order directing a federal agency to prioritize enforcement of a particular regulation, without any new legislation from Congress. Classify this action, and explain its constitutional basis.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">This is an example of <strong>informal presidential power</strong>. Executive orders are not explicitly listed in Article II's text — instead, presidents have historically justified them as an extension of the general \"executive power\" and \"take care that the laws be faithfully executed\" clauses, using them to direct how existing executive branch agencies carry out their duties. Because this authority rests on a broader, more contested constitutional interpretation rather than an explicit grant, executive orders can be challenged in court or reversed by future presidents far more easily than a formally-passed law.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Distinguish formal presidential powers (explicit in Article II) from informal powers (developed through practice).</li>\n      <li>Explain Hamilton's argument in Federalist No. 70 for a single, \"energetic\" executive.</li>\n      <li>Explain why executive orders, as informal powers, are more legally fragile than statutory law.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s24\"><span class=\"num\">2.4</span>Checks on Presidential Power</h2>\n  <p>Despite the growth of presidential power described in the previous section, the president remains subject to substantial checks from both Congress and the courts — checks and balances didn't stop applying just because the presidency expanded its informal reach.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Congress Checks the President Through Both Formal and Practical Tools</span>\n    <p>Congress's formal checks on the president include overriding a veto (2/3 vote in both chambers), refusing to confirm nominations, refusing to ratify treaties, and the ultimate check — impeachment (a majority vote in the House) and removal from office (2/3 vote in the Senate). Beyond these formal tools, Congress also holds significant PRACTICAL leverage through the power of the purse: since most executive branch action requires funding that only Congress can appropriate, Congress can meaningfully constrain presidential action simply by controlling the budget, even without a direct confrontation over a specific policy.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — The War Powers Resolution Formally Limits Military Deployment</span>\n    <p>The <strong>War Powers Resolution (1973)</strong>, passed over President Nixon's veto in response to executive overreach in Vietnam, requires the president to notify Congress within <strong>48 hours</strong> of committing armed forces to hostilities, and limits deployment to <strong>60 days</strong> (plus a 30-day withdrawal period) without specific congressional authorization. In practice, presidents of both parties have contested its constitutionality and often treated its requirements as more advisory than binding, so it illustrates both a formal check on the president's commander-in-chief power and the limits of that check when a president is willing to test it.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>Impeachment and removal are two SEPARATE steps, and conflating them is a very common error. The House impeaches (a formal accusation, requiring only a simple majority) — this is roughly analogous to an indictment, not a conviction. The Senate then holds a trial and votes on removal, which requires a 2/3 supermajority. A president can be impeached by the House without ever being removed by the Senate — impeachment alone does not remove a president from office.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 4</span>\n    <p><strong>Tracing the Impeachment Process.</strong> Explain the two distinct steps required to remove a president from office, and the vote threshold required at each step.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">Step one: the <strong>House of Representatives</strong> votes to impeach, requiring only a <strong>simple majority</strong> — this is a formal accusation of wrongdoing, comparable to an indictment, and does not by itself remove the president. Step two: the <strong>Senate</strong> holds a trial on the impeachment charges and votes on conviction and removal, requiring a <strong>2/3 supermajority</strong> to actually remove the president from office. Both steps must occur, in that order, for removal to happen — impeachment by the House alone leaves the president in office.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>List the formal congressional checks on presidential power, including the correct vote thresholds for each.</li>\n      <li>Explain the power of the purse as a practical (not just formal) check on presidential action.</li>\n      <li>Correctly distinguish impeachment (House, simple majority) from removal (Senate, 2/3 vote) as two separate steps.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s25\"><span class=\"num\">2.5</span>The Judicial Branch: Structure and Judicial Review</h2>\n  <p>The federal judiciary is structured as a three-tiered system, and its single most important power — judicial review — isn't explicitly written into the Constitution at all. It was established by the Supreme Court itself, in one of the most consequential rulings in American history.</p>\n\n  <table class=\"formula-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>U.S. District Courts</td><td>Trial courts — where federal cases are first heard, evidence presented, facts established</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>U.S. Courts of Appeals (Circuit Courts)</td><td>Review district court decisions for legal errors; no new trials or evidence</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>U.S. Supreme Court</td><td>Highest court; mostly discretionary jurisdiction — chooses which cases to hear via a writ of certiorari</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Marbury v. Madison Created Judicial Review</span>\n    <p><strong>Marbury v. Madison (1803)</strong>, a required case, established <strong>judicial review</strong> — the Supreme Court's power to declare a law or executive action unconstitutional. This power appears nowhere explicitly in the Constitution's text; Chief Justice John Marshall's opinion reasoned that since the Constitution is the supreme law of the land, and it's the judiciary's role to interpret the law, courts must have the authority to strike down laws that conflict with the Constitution. This single ruling transformed the judicial branch from what Hamilton, in Federalist No. 78, had called the \"least dangerous branch\" into a co-equal check on the other two.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>The Supreme Court does not have to hear every case appealed to it — in fact, it hears only a small fraction of cases it's asked to review. A party seeking Supreme Court review files a petition for a \"writ of certiorari,\" and the Court grants review only if at least four of the nine justices agree to hear it (the \"rule of four\"). Don't assume losing at a circuit court automatically guarantees a Supreme Court hearing — the vast majority of certiorari petitions are denied, leaving the lower court's ruling as the final word.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 5</span>\n    <p><strong>Explaining Marbury v. Madison's Significance.</strong> Explain why Marbury v. Madison is considered foundational to the modern power of the judicial branch, even though the case itself involved a relatively minor dispute over a judicial appointment.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">While the immediate dispute in Marbury v. Madison was narrow (whether William Marbury was entitled to a judicial commission), Chief Justice Marshall used the case to establish a far broader and more consequential principle: that the Supreme Court has the authority to review acts of Congress and the executive branch, and strike them down if they conflict with the Constitution. This power — judicial review — is nowhere explicitly stated in the constitutional text, but it has become the foundation of the judiciary's role as a genuine check on the other two branches ever since, making Marbury v. Madison foundational despite the narrowness of the original dispute.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Describe the three-tiered structure of the federal court system and each level's function.</li>\n      <li>Explain what Marbury v. Madison established, and why judicial review isn't explicitly stated in the Constitution's text.</li>\n      <li>Explain the writ of certiorari and the \"rule of four\" governing which cases the Supreme Court chooses to hear.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s26\"><span class=\"num\">2.6</span>Judicial Decision-Making and Legitimacy</h2>\n  <p>Unlike Congress or the presidency, the judicial branch has no army and no direct control over funding — Hamilton's \"least dangerous branch.\" Its authority rests almost entirely on the public's willingness to accept and follow its rulings, which makes judicial legitimacy a genuinely fragile and important concept.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Stare Decisis and the Value of Predictability</span>\n    <p><strong>Stare decisis</strong> (\"to stand by things decided\") is the principle that courts generally follow established precedent from prior rulings rather than reconsidering settled legal questions from scratch each time. This principle supports judicial legitimacy by making the law more predictable and stable, and by making the Court appear less like a body simply imposing the personal preferences of whichever justices currently sit on it. Courts do sometimes overturn precedent — but doing so is understood as a significant and closely scrutinized departure from the norm, precisely because stare decisis is so central to how the judiciary maintains public trust.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>Federal judges, including Supreme Court justices, serve during \"good behavior\" — effectively life tenure — specifically to insulate them from short-term political pressure and public opinion, allowing them to rule based on legal judgment rather than fear of losing their position. Don't describe judicial independence as making the courts entirely immune from checks, though: judges can still be impeached and removed for serious misconduct, and Congress retains some authority to structure the federal court system (such as the number of justices), even though it cannot remove a judge simply for disagreeing with a ruling.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 6</span>\n    <p><strong>Explaining Judicial Legitimacy.</strong> Explain why public compliance with Supreme Court rulings matters more for the judicial branch than for the other two branches of government.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">Unlike Congress (which can pass binding statutes) or the president (who commands the military and directs federal law enforcement), the judicial branch has no independent enforcement mechanism of its own — it cannot compel compliance with its rulings through force or funding. Its authority instead depends almost entirely on the other branches and the public voluntarily accepting its rulings as legitimate and binding. This is exactly why Hamilton described the judiciary as the \"least dangerous branch\" in Federalist No. 78 — its power rests on judgment and perceived legitimacy rather than the \"sword\" (executive enforcement) or the \"purse\" (legislative funding) that the other branches directly control.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Explain the principle of stare decisis and how it supports judicial legitimacy.</li>\n      <li>Explain why lifetime tenure for federal judges is designed to promote judicial independence.</li>\n      <li>Explain why the judicial branch's authority depends more heavily on public and institutional compliance than the other two branches.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s27\"><span class=\"num\">2.7</span>The Federal Bureaucracy</h2>\n  <p>The federal bureaucracy — the collection of executive branch departments, agencies, and independent commissions that actually implement and enforce the laws Congress passes — is sometimes informally called the \"fourth branch\" of government, despite not being explicitly described that way in the Constitution.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — The Bureaucracy Does More Than Just \"Follow Orders\"</span>\n    <p>While the bureaucracy's core function is implementation — carrying out laws passed by Congress and signed by the president — agencies also engage in <strong>rule-making</strong>, a quasi-legislative process where they issue detailed regulations that carry the force of law, filling in the specifics that broader statutes leave undefined. This gives unelected bureaucratic agencies substantial practical influence over policy outcomes, since the specific rules an agency writes can significantly shape how a law actually functions in practice, well beyond what the original statutory text explicitly specified.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Iron Triangles and Issue Networks</span>\n    <p>Bureaucratic policy-making doesn't happen in isolation — it's shaped by ongoing relationships between a congressional committee (which controls the agency's authorizing legislation and budget), the bureaucratic agency itself (which writes and enforces the rules), and an interested group (which lobbies both and often provides technical expertise or political support). This mutually reinforcing three-way relationship is called an <strong>iron triangle</strong> — each part helps the others achieve their goals, making the relationship stable and hard for outsiders to disrupt. A looser, more modern variant is the <strong>issue network</strong>: a broader, more fluid set of participants (agencies, committees, interest groups, think tanks, media, academic experts) that forms around a policy issue without the same tight, closed relationship an iron triangle implies.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>The federal bureaucracy is not a unified, single-minded entity — it consists of cabinet departments, independent regulatory agencies, and government corporations, each with somewhat different degrees of independence from direct presidential control. Cabinet department heads generally serve at the president's pleasure and can be removed relatively easily; independent regulatory agency heads often have fixed terms and more insulation from direct presidential removal, specifically to reduce short-term political influence over technical regulatory decisions.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 7</span>\n    <p><strong>Bureaucratic Rule-Making.</strong> Congress passes a law requiring workplaces to maintain \"safe\" conditions, without defining specific safety standards. Explain how the bureaucracy would typically fill in this gap, and why this matters for the balance of power between branches.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">A relevant federal agency (for instance, one focused on workplace safety) would engage in <strong>rule-making</strong> — issuing detailed, legally binding regulations that define specific, measurable safety standards employers must meet. This matters for the balance of power because it means an UNELECTED agency, not Congress itself, ends up making many of the substantive policy decisions that actually determine how the law functions in practice — a significant delegation of practical policymaking authority from the legislative branch to the bureaucracy, even though Congress retains oversight authority and can revise the underlying statute if it disagrees with how an agency has implemented it.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Explain the bureaucracy's implementation function and its quasi-legislative rule-making function.</li>\n      <li>Distinguish cabinet departments from independent regulatory agencies in terms of presidential control.</li>\n      <li>Explain why bureaucratic rule-making represents a meaningful delegation of policymaking power.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s28\"><span class=\"num\">2.8</span>Policy-Making Across the Branches</h2>\n  <p>This final section of the unit ties everything together: turning an idea into actual, implemented federal policy requires passing through all three branches (plus the bureaucracy), with real opportunities for any of them to shape, slow, or block the outcome along the way.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Policy-Making Is a Multi-Stage Process, Not a Single Event</span>\n    <p>A typical policy's path runs roughly: Congress drafts and passes legislation (often shaped heavily by committee work) → the president signs or vetoes it → if signed, the relevant executive agency writes implementing regulations through rule-making → affected parties may challenge the law or the regulations in court, potentially reaching the Supreme Court for a final ruling on constitutionality. At virtually every stage, there's an opportunity for a different branch (or a different set of political actors) to reshape the outcome — which is exactly why policy-making in the American system tends to be slow, incremental, and full of compromise, rather than swift and decisive. This effect is magnified under <strong>divided government</strong> — when different parties control the presidency and at least one chamber of Congress — since the president and congressional majority then have weaker incentives to cooperate, often producing <strong>gridlock</strong>: a state in which little or no significant legislation can pass because no single actor or party controls enough of the process to move it forward alone.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 8</span>\n    <p><strong>Tracing a Policy's Path.</strong> Trace the path a new environmental regulation might take from initial legislation to final implementation, identifying which branch is responsible at each stage.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">Congress (legislative branch) drafts and passes a statute establishing a general environmental policy goal. The president (executive branch) signs it into law. A relevant federal agency, part of the bureaucracy (also within the executive branch), then engages in rule-making to establish specific, detailed regulatory standards implementing the statute's broad goal. If an affected business or advocacy group believes the resulting regulation exceeds the agency's statutory authority or violates the Constitution, they can challenge it in federal court (judicial branch), potentially all the way to the Supreme Court, which has final say on the regulation's constitutionality and legal validity.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Trace the multi-stage process of policy-making from legislation through implementation and potential judicial review.</li>\n      <li>Identify which branch (or part of the bureaucracy) is responsible at each stage of that process.</li>\n      <li>Explain why this multi-stage structure tends to produce slow, incremental policy change rather than swift, decisive action.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"practice\">Unit 2 Practice Set</h2>\n  <p>Work through every problem before checking the answer key.</p>\n\n  <h3>Conceptual</h3>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">1</span><span>Explain why a bill can have majority support in the Senate (say, 55 votes) and still fail to pass.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">2</span><span>Distinguish the trustee and delegate models of representation.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">3</span><span>Explain the difference between impeachment and removal, including which chamber handles each and the vote threshold required.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">4</span><span>Explain why Marbury v. Madison is considered foundational to the judicial branch's modern power.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">5</span><span>Explain why bureaucratic rule-making is sometimes described as \"quasi-legislative.\"</span></div>\n\n  <h3>Application</h3>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">6</span><span>A state draws a congressional district with wildly unequal population compared to other districts in the state. Which required case is most relevant to challenging this district?</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">7</span><span>A state draws a district using race as the predominant factor, aiming to increase minority representation. Which required case is most relevant to challenging this district?</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">8</span><span>A president issues an executive order that a federal court later strikes down as exceeding presidential authority. Explain why this outcome is possible, given that executive orders are a real presidential tool.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">9</span><span>Congress refuses to appropriate funding for a program the president wants to expand. Identify the type of check being exercised.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">10</span><span>A losing party appeals their case to the Supreme Court, but the Court declines to hear it. Explain what this means for the lower court's ruling.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">11</span><span>Explain how Federalist No. 70 justifies having a single president rather than an executive council.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">12</span><span>A federal agency issues a detailed regulation implementing a broadly-worded law passed by Congress. Identify the bureaucratic function being exercised.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">13</span><span>Explain why lifetime judicial tenure is designed to promote judicial independence, and identify one check that still applies to federal judges despite this tenure.</span></div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"margin-top:30px;\">Answer Key</h3>\n  <div class=\"answer-key\">\n    <details><summary>Question 1</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Because ending Senate debate (invoking cloture) on most legislation requires 60 votes, not a simple majority — a bill with 55 votes can still be blocked by a filibuster, since it falls short of the 60 needed to force a final vote.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 2</summary><div class=\"a-content\">A trustee uses their own independent judgment, even against constituents' stated wishes. A delegate directly follows constituents' expressed preferences, even against their own personal judgment.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 3</summary><div class=\"a-content\">The House impeaches (a formal accusation) with only a simple majority vote. The Senate then holds a trial and votes on removal, requiring a 2/3 supermajority. Both steps are required for actual removal from office; impeachment alone does not remove a president.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 4</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Marbury v. Madison established judicial review — the Supreme Court's power to strike down laws or executive actions as unconstitutional — a power not explicitly stated in the Constitution's text, but one that has become the foundation of the judiciary's role as a genuine check on the other two branches.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 5</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Because rule-making allows unelected agencies to issue detailed regulations that carry the force of law, filling in specifics that Congress's broader statutory language leaves undefined — functionally similar to lawmaking, even though it's carried out by the executive branch's bureaucracy rather than the legislature itself.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 6</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Baker v. Carr, which established the \"one person, one vote\" principle requiring districts to have roughly equal populations, and made redistricting cases justiciable (reviewable by federal courts) in the first place.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 7</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Shaw v. Reno, which established that districts drawn with race as the predominant factor are subject to strict scrutiny and may violate the Equal Protection Clause, even when the goal is increasing minority representation.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 8</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Executive orders are an informal power, not explicitly granted by the Constitution's text — they are typically justified through a broader reading of executive authority, which means they can be challenged in court and struck down if a judge finds the president exceeded their legitimate constitutional authority.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 9</summary><div class=\"a-content\">This is Congress exercising the power of the purse — a practical (not just formal) check on presidential and executive branch action, since most programs require congressional appropriation of funds to operate.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 10</summary><div class=\"a-content\">The lower court's ruling stands as the final word in that case. The Supreme Court hears only a small fraction of cases appealed to it (via the discretionary writ of certiorari and the \"rule of four\"), and declining to hear a case leaves the previous ruling in place.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 11</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Hamilton argues a single executive provides \"energy\" — decisiveness and the ability to act quickly, especially in a crisis — along with clearer accountability, since a single person cannot hide behind shared blame the way a multi-member council could.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 12</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Rule-making — the quasi-legislative process by which bureaucratic agencies issue detailed, legally binding regulations to implement broader statutory goals set by Congress.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 13</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Lifetime tenure (\"during good behavior\") insulates judges from short-term political pressure and the fear of losing their position, allowing rulings based on legal judgment rather than public opinion. Despite this, judges can still be impeached and removed by Congress for serious misconduct.</div></details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"footer-nav\">\n    Next up: head to the <strong>Unit 2 QBank</strong> to drill these ideas, then move on to <strong>Unit 3 — Civil Liberties and Civil Rights</strong>.\n  </div>\n\n</div>\n",
  "3": "<div class=\"hero\">\n  <div class=\"eyebrow\">Peters Family Homeschool · AP U.S. Government &amp; Politics</div>\n  <h1>Civil Liberties and Civil Rights</h1>\n  <p class=\"sub\">This unit carries more required Supreme Court cases than any other, and for good reason: it's where the Bill of Rights stops being a list of promises on paper and becomes a living set of rules, shaped case by case by real disputes between individuals and government. Precision matters enormously here — knowing roughly what a case was about isn't the same as knowing its actual holding.</p>\n  <div class=\"hero-pills\">\n    <span class=\"pill\">Exam weight 13–18%</span>\n    <span class=\"pill\">7 topics</span>\n    <span class=\"pill\">65 practice questions</span>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"toc\">\n  <div class=\"toc-label\">Jump to a section</div>\n  <ol>\n    <li><a href=\"#s31\">3.1 The Bill of Rights and Selective Incorporation</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s32\">3.2 Freedom of Religion</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s33\">3.3 Freedom of Speech</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s34\">3.4 Freedom of the Press</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s35\">3.5 Due Process and the Right to Privacy</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s36\">3.6 The 14th Amendment and the Civil Rights Movement</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s37\">3.7 Balancing Liberty, Equality, and Order</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#practice\">Practice Set</a></li>\n  </ol>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"wrap\">\n\n  <div class=\"skillbox\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label skill\">Master This First — Civil Liberties vs. Civil Rights</span>\n    <p>These two terms get used almost interchangeably in casual speech, but the AP exam expects you to keep them distinct. <strong>Civil liberties</strong> are protections FROM government — limits on what government can do to you (freedom of speech, freedom of religion, protection from unreasonable searches). <strong>Civil rights</strong> are protections TO equal treatment — guarantees of equal treatment BY government, especially against discrimination (the right to vote regardless of race, equal protection under the law). Most of the First Amendment is about civil liberties; the 14th Amendment's Equal Protection Clause and the Civil Rights Movement are about civil rights. Keep asking \"is this about government leaving people alone, or about government treating people equally?\" — that question sorts almost everything in this unit correctly.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Quick Reference — Every Required Case in This Unit, One Line Each</span>\n    <p>This unit alone covers 9 of the course's 15 required Supreme Court cases — more than any other unit. Before diving into the sections below, use this table as your anchor point. Come back to it after each section and try to reconstruct the holding from the case name alone, without looking — that's the actual skill the SCOTUS Comparison FRQ tests.</p>\n    <table class=\"formula-table\" style=\"margin:10px 0 0;\">\n      <tr><th>Case (Year)</th><th>Amendment / Clause</th><th>One-Line Holding</th></tr>\n      <tr><td>Engel v. Vitale (1962)</td><td>1st Amdt. — Establishment</td><td>School-sponsored prayer is unconstitutional, even if voluntary and nondenominational.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Wisconsin v. Yoder (1972)</td><td>1st Amdt. — Free Exercise</td><td>Compulsory education can't override Amish families' sincere religious practice.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Schenck v. U.S. (1919)</td><td>1st Amdt. — Speech</td><td>Established \"clear and present danger\"; upheld conviction for anti-draft leaflets.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Tinker v. Des Moines (1969)</td><td>1st Amdt. — Speech</td><td>Symbolic student speech protected unless it causes \"substantial disruption.\"</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>N.Y. Times Co. v. U.S. (1971)</td><td>1st Amdt. — Press</td><td>Government failed to justify prior restraint of the Pentagon Papers.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Gideon v. Wainwright (1963)</td><td>6th Amdt. — Counsel</td><td>States must provide a public defender to defendants who can't afford one.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>McDonald v. Chicago (2010)</td><td>2nd Amdt. — Bear Arms</td><td>Incorporated the right to bear arms to state and local governments.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Roe v. Wade (1973)</td><td>14th Amdt. — Due Process</td><td>Right to privacy extended to abortion decision (later overturned by Dobbs, 2022).</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Brown v. Board (1954)</td><td>14th Amdt. — Equal Protection</td><td>Segregated schools are inherently unequal; overturned \"separate but equal.\"</td></tr>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s31\"><span class=\"num\">3.1</span>The Bill of Rights and Selective Incorporation</h2>\n  <p>The Bill of Rights, as originally written and ratified, only restricted the FEDERAL government — it said nothing about what state governments could do. Selective incorporation is the process by which that changed, gradually and case by case, over more than a century.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Incorporation Happens One Right at a Time, Through the 14th Amendment</span>\n    <p>The 14th Amendment's Due Process Clause (\"nor shall any State deprive any person of life, liberty, or property, without due process of law\") became the vehicle courts used to apply specific Bill of Rights protections to STATE governments as well — but this happened incrementally, right by right, case by case, rather than all at once. <strong>Gideon v. Wainwright (1963)</strong>, a required case, incorporated the 6th Amendment right to counsel, ruling that states must provide a public defender to criminal defendants who cannot afford their own attorney. <strong>McDonald v. Chicago (2010)</strong>, another required case, incorporated the 2nd Amendment right to bear arms, ruling that a Chicago handgun ban violated a right that applies to state and local governments, not just the federal government.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>Not every provision of the Bill of Rights has been incorporated to apply to the states — incorporation happened (and continues to happen) through individual court rulings, one right at a time, not through a single sweeping decision. Don't assume a right automatically applies to state governments just because it's in the Bill of Rights; you need an actual incorporation ruling establishing that specific right applies to the states, which is exactly why Gideon and McDonald are individually significant, separate precedents rather than a single blanket rule.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 1</span>\n    <p><strong>Applying Selective Incorporation.</strong> A state passes a law banning handgun ownership within city limits. A resident challenges the law, citing the 2nd Amendment. Explain the relevant precedent and its likely bearing on this case.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">The relevant precedent is <strong>McDonald v. Chicago (2010)</strong>, which incorporated the 2nd Amendment right to bear arms via the 14th Amendment's Due Process Clause, establishing that this right applies to state and local governments, not just the federal government. Based on this precedent, the resident would have a strong argument that the city's handgun ban is subject to 2nd Amendment scrutiny — the same as if the federal government had passed the ban — since McDonald established that state and local governments cannot violate this right any more than the federal government can.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Explain why the Bill of Rights originally applied only to the federal government.</li>\n      <li>Explain how the 14th Amendment's Due Process Clause serves as the vehicle for selective incorporation.</li>\n      <li>State the specific holdings of Gideon v. Wainwright and McDonald v. Chicago as incorporation cases.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s32\"><span class=\"num\">3.2</span>Freedom of Religion</h2>\n  <p>The First Amendment contains two distinct religion clauses that sometimes pull in different directions: the Establishment Clause (government cannot promote or favor religion) and the Free Exercise Clause (government cannot unduly restrict individuals' religious practice).</p>\n\n  <table class=\"formula-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Clause</th><th>Protects Against</th><th>Required Case</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Establishment Clause</td><td>Government establishing, promoting, or favoring a religion</td><td>Engel v. Vitale (1962)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Free Exercise Clause</td><td>Government restricting an individual's religious practice</td><td>Wisconsin v. Yoder (1972)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Engel v. Vitale: No School-Sponsored Prayer</span>\n    <p><strong>Engel v. Vitale (1962)</strong> ruled that a New York school district's practice of having students recite a short, voluntary, nondenominational prayer at the start of the school day violated the Establishment Clause. The Court reasoned that it is not the government's place to compose or sponsor an official prayer, even a nondenominational one, and even when student participation is technically voluntary — the government's OFFICIAL ENDORSEMENT of the practice was itself the constitutional problem, not just whether individual students were forced to participate.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Wisconsin v. Yoder: Protecting Religious Practice from Government Burden</span>\n    <p><strong>Wisconsin v. Yoder (1972)</strong> ruled that Amish parents could not be compelled, under the state's compulsory education law, to send their children to formal schooling beyond the 8th grade, since doing so imposed a substantial burden on their sincerely held religious beliefs and way of life. The Court weighed the state's interest in universal education against the burden on free exercise, and found the state's interest insufficient to override the Amish families' free exercise rights in this specific case. (For additional context beyond Engel: later Establishment Clause cases developed other analytical frameworks, including the Lemon test's purpose/effect/entanglement analysis, since largely displaced by a \"historical practices and understandings\" approach in <em>Kennedy v. Bremerton School District</em>, 2022 — enrichment beyond required-case content, not itself tested directly.)</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>Engel v. Vitale and Wisconsin v. Yoder involve two DIFFERENT religion clauses, and it's easy to mix them up under time pressure. Engel is an ESTABLISHMENT Clause case (government cannot promote religious practice, even voluntary prayer). Yoder is a FREE EXERCISE Clause case (government cannot unduly burden an individual's ability to practice their own religion). A quick way to keep them straight: Engel is about the government ADDING religion where it shouldn't; Yoder is about the government BLOCKING religious practice it shouldn't block.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 2</span>\n    <p><strong>Establishment vs. Free Exercise.</strong> A public school requires all students to participate in a daily moment of teacher-led prayer. Separately, a state law requires all children, without religious exemption, to receive a vaccination the child's family religiously objects to. Identify which clause is implicated in each scenario.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">The school prayer scenario implicates the <strong>Establishment Clause</strong> — government (the public school) is actively sponsoring and promoting a specific religious practice, closely paralleling Engel v. Vitale. The vaccination scenario implicates the <strong>Free Exercise Clause</strong> — government is imposing a legal requirement that burdens a family's religious practice, closely paralleling the type of claim raised (though not identically resolved) in Wisconsin v. Yoder.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Distinguish the Establishment Clause from the Free Exercise Clause.</li>\n      <li>State the holding of Engel v. Vitale and explain why voluntary participation didn't resolve the constitutional problem.</li>\n      <li>State the holding of Wisconsin v. Yoder and explain the balancing test the Court applied.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">Q</span><span>A public school board votes to open every school day with a brief, teacher-led prayer, and tells parents any student may step into the hallway instead. Is this most likely constitutional?</span></div>\n  <div class=\"answer-key\"><details><summary>Check your answer</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Almost certainly not. This is essentially the Engel v. Vitale fact pattern again — a government-sponsored, official prayer — and the presence of an opt-out doesn't cure the problem, since the constitutional violation is the school's official sponsorship of the practice itself, not whether any individual student is forced to take part.</div></details></div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s33\"><span class=\"num\">3.3</span>Freedom of Speech</h2>\n  <p>Freedom of speech is rarely treated as an absolute, unlimited right — the Supreme Court has developed specific tests over time for when and how government may restrict speech, and two required cases define the core boundaries you need to know.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Schenck v. United States and the \"Clear and Present Danger\" Test</span>\n    <p><strong>Schenck v. United States (1919)</strong> upheld the conviction of a man who distributed leaflets urging resistance to the World War I military draft, ruling that speech creating a \"clear and present danger\" of bringing about substantive evils that Congress has a right to prevent may be restricted — speech protections are not absolute, especially during wartime, when the government's interest in restricting certain speech is at its strongest. This case established the \"clear and present danger\" standard as an early framework for limiting First Amendment protection.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Tinker v. Des Moines and Symbolic Speech in Schools</span>\n    <p><strong>Tinker v. Des Moines (1969)</strong> ruled that students wearing black armbands to school to silently protest the Vietnam War were engaged in protected symbolic speech under the First Amendment — famously stating that students do not \"shed their constitutional rights to freedom of speech or expression at the schoolhouse gate.\" The Court established that schools may restrict student speech only when it would substantially disrupt the educational environment or infringe on the rights of others — mere discomfort or disagreement with the speech's message isn't enough to justify restricting it.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>Schenck v. United States remains a required case, and you should know its facts and its \"clear and present danger\" test precisely — but that test is no longer the operative standard for incitement-type speech. In <strong>Brandenburg v. Ohio (1969)</strong>, a non-required but useful-context case, the Court replaced it with the \"<strong>imminent lawless action</strong>\" test: advocacy of illegal conduct is protected unless it is directed toward inciting imminent lawless action and is likely to actually produce it. When discussing Schenck on the exam, describe its original holding and test accurately (that's what's tested), while understanding it no longer represents current incitement doctrine.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>Tinker's \"substantial disruption\" standard is specifically about STUDENT speech in a SCHOOL setting — it's a more permissive standard for student expression than a general First Amendment analysis might otherwise apply outside of schools, but it's still more protective of speech than a school administrator's simple discomfort or disagreement would justify. Don't confuse \"substantial disruption\" with \"any disruption at all,\" and don't confuse it with Schenck's \"clear and present danger\" test, which applies in a different context (speech that might incite illegal action or undermine a specific, pressing government interest, historically applied outside the school context).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 3</span>\n    <p><strong>Applying the \"Substantial Disruption\" Standard.</strong> A student wears a t-shirt with a political message to school. Some classmates express mild disagreement, but no disruption to class activities occurs. Using Tinker v. Des Moines, evaluate whether the school could constitutionally punish the student for wearing the shirt.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">Based on Tinker's standard, the school likely could NOT constitutionally punish the student. Tinker protects student symbolic speech unless it causes a SUBSTANTIAL disruption to the educational environment or infringes on others' rights — mere disagreement or discomfort among classmates, without any actual disruption to school activities, falls well short of that threshold. The t-shirt closely parallels the armbands in Tinker itself: a passive, non-disruptive form of political expression that the Court found constitutionally protected.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>State the \"clear and present danger\" test from Schenck v. United States and its context.</li>\n      <li>State the \"substantial disruption\" standard from Tinker v. Des Moines as it applies specifically to student speech.</li>\n      <li>Apply the substantial disruption standard to a new scenario involving student expression.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">Q</span><span>A group of students silently kneels during the school's morning announcements to protest a policy issue. No class is interrupted. A different student loudly shouts over a teacher's lecture to make a political point, forcing class to stop for ten minutes. Which student(s), if any, would likely be protected under Tinker v. Des Moines?</span></div>\n  <div class=\"answer-key\"><details><summary>Check your answer</summary><div class=\"a-content\">The kneeling students are almost certainly protected — silent, non-disruptive symbolic speech is exactly what Tinker protects. The shouting student is not protected under Tinker's standard, since forcing class to actually stop for ten minutes is a real substantial disruption to the educational environment, not merely disagreement or discomfort. Notice both scenarios involve \"political expression,\" but only one crosses Tinker's specific disruption threshold.</div></details></div>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Content-Based vs. Content-Neutral Restrictions, and Categorically Unprotected Speech</span>\n    <p>Government restrictions on speech fall into a few recognizable categories: (1) <strong>content-based restrictions</strong>, which target speech because of its message or subject matter, and are subject to strict scrutiny; (2) <strong>content-neutral time, place, and manner restrictions</strong>, which regulate WHEN, WHERE, or HOW speech occurs without regard to its message (e.g., a noise ordinance or a parade permit requirement) — these are constitutional as long as they are narrowly tailored, serve a significant government interest, and leave open ample alternative channels of communication; and (3) categories of speech the Court has held receive <strong>no First Amendment protection at all</strong>, including obscenity, \"fighting words,\" true threats, and incitement of imminent lawless action (the Brandenburg standard noted above).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 3b</span>\n    <p><strong>Applying Time/Place/Manner Analysis.</strong> A city requires all groups to obtain a permit before holding a parade on public streets, and applies the same permit requirements and fees regardless of the parade's political message. A group denied a permit due to a scheduling conflict argues its free speech rights were violated. Evaluate this claim.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">Because the permit requirement applies equally regardless of the parade's message, it is a <strong>content-neutral time, place, and manner restriction</strong>, not a content-based one — so it does not trigger strict scrutiny. Instead, a court would ask whether the requirement is narrowly tailored to serve a significant government interest (like traffic and public-safety coordination) and leaves open ample alternative channels for the group's expression (such as a different date or another public space). If those conditions are met, the restriction is likely constitutional even though it denied this specific group a permit for this specific date.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s34\"><span class=\"num\">3.4</span>Freedom of the Press</h2>\n  <p>A free press is widely understood as essential to a functioning democracy, since it allows citizens to access information needed to hold government accountable — and this section's required case addresses one of the most direct threats to press freedom: government attempts to block publication before it even happens.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — New York Times Co. v. United States and Prior Restraint</span>\n    <p><strong>New York Times Co. v. United States (1971)</strong> — commonly known as the \"Pentagon Papers\" case — addressed the government's attempt to obtain a court injunction blocking newspapers from publishing classified documents about U.S. involvement in the Vietnam War. The Supreme Court ruled against the government, holding that it had failed to meet the extremely heavy burden required to justify <strong>prior restraint</strong> (government action blocking speech or publication BEFORE it occurs, rather than punishing it afterward). This case established a strong presumption against prior restraint, treating it as one of the most serious and disfavored types of First Amendment violations.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>New York Times Co. v. United States is specifically about PRIOR RESTRAINT — stopping publication before it happens — not about whether the newspapers could later face legal consequences AFTER publishing (a separate legal question the ruling didn't fully resolve). Don't conflate \"the government couldn't block publication in advance\" with \"the newspapers faced no legal risk whatsoever from publishing\" — the case's holding is narrower and more specific than a blanket statement that classified information can always be freely published without any consequence.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 4</span>\n    <p><strong>Identifying Prior Restraint.</strong> A government agency seeks a court order preventing a newspaper from publishing a story about an ongoing investigation, before the story is ever printed. Using New York Times Co. v. United States, evaluate the likely outcome.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">This scenario is a direct example of the government seeking <strong>prior restraint</strong> — blocking publication before it happens. Under the precedent of New York Times Co. v. United States, the government faces an extremely heavy burden to justify such an order, and courts are highly skeptical of prior restraint as a First Amendment matter. Absent an extraordinarily compelling justification (of the kind the government failed to establish even in the Pentagon Papers case itself), a court would likely decline to block publication in advance, though the newspaper could still potentially face separate legal consequences after publication if the story violated some other law.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Define prior restraint and explain why it is treated as a particularly serious First Amendment concern.</li>\n      <li>State the holding of New York Times Co. v. United States and the heavy burden it places on government seeking prior restraint.</li>\n      <li>Distinguish blocking publication in advance from potential legal consequences after publication.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s35\"><span class=\"num\">3.5</span>Due Process and the Right to Privacy</h2>\n  <p>The right to privacy is a genuinely interesting case study in constitutional interpretation: it isn't explicitly named anywhere in the Bill of Rights' text, but courts have found it implied through the 14th Amendment's Due Process Clause and other constitutional provisions — a concept sometimes called \"substantive due process.\"</p>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Roe v. Wade and the Right to Privacy</span>\n    <p><strong>Roe v. Wade (1973)</strong> ruled that the constitutional right to privacy, grounded in the 14th Amendment's Due Process Clause, extended to a woman's decision whether to have an abortion, subject to a framework balancing that right against the state's legitimate interests as pregnancy progressed. This case is a foundational example of substantive due process — using the Due Process Clause to protect rights not explicitly enumerated in the Constitution's text.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>Roe v. Wade remains a required case for this course specifically because of its historical and doctrinal significance to the right to privacy — but it is important to know that <strong>Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization (2022)</strong> overturned Roe v. Wade, ruling that the Constitution does not confer a right to abortion and returning the regulation of abortion to individual states. When discussing Roe on the exam, be precise: describe its ORIGINAL holding and reasoning accurately, since that's what the required-case content tests, while understanding that it is no longer the controlling precedent today. Getting this sequence backward — or treating Roe as still governing law — is a factual error worth avoiding.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 5</span>\n    <p><strong>Explaining Substantive Due Process.</strong> Explain how the right to privacy can be constitutionally protected even though the word \"privacy\" does not appear anywhere in the Constitution's text.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">Courts have interpreted the 14th Amendment's Due Process Clause — which protects \"life, liberty, and property\" from government deprivation without due process — as protecting certain fundamental rights not explicitly listed in the constitutional text, through a doctrine called <strong>substantive due process</strong>. Rather than reading \"due process\" as purely procedural (fair legal procedures), courts have read it to also protect certain substantive liberties the Court considers fundamental to personal autonomy, including a right to privacy. This is exactly the reasoning used in Roe v. Wade to ground the right to privacy in the Due Process Clause, despite the absence of the word \"privacy\" from the Constitution's actual text.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Explain substantive due process and how it can protect rights not explicitly named in the Constitution.</li>\n      <li>State the original holding of Roe v. Wade accurately.</li>\n      <li>Explain that Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization (2022) later overturned Roe v. Wade.</li>\n      <li>Identify the core procedural protection of the 4th, 5th, 6th, and 8th Amendments, and give one required or well-known case tied to each.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Procedural Due Process: The 4th, 5th, 6th, and 8th Amendments</span>\n    <p>Beyond the substantive due process covered above, \"due process\" also centrally means the specific <strong>procedural</strong> protections the Bill of Rights gives people accused of crimes:</p>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>4th Amendment</strong> — protects against unreasonable search and seizure. Government generally needs a warrant, supported by <strong>probable cause</strong>, before searching a person's property where they have a reasonable expectation of privacy. Evidence obtained in violation of this protection is generally inadmissible in court under the <strong>exclusionary rule</strong> (established in <em>Mapp v. Ohio</em>, 1961, a non-required but highly relevant case).</li>\n      <li><strong>5th Amendment</strong> — protects against compelled <strong>self-incrimination</strong> (the basis for the familiar <em>Miranda v. Arizona</em> warnings, non-required but standard exam content), bars <strong>double jeopardy</strong> (being tried twice for the same offense), requires a <strong>grand jury indictment</strong> for serious federal charges (this specific requirement has not been incorporated to the states), and includes the <strong>Takings Clause</strong> (government must pay just compensation when it takes private property for public use).</li>\n      <li><strong>6th Amendment</strong> — guarantees the right to counsel (see Gideon v. Wainwright, 3.1), a speedy and public trial, an impartial jury, and the right to confront witnesses against you.</li>\n      <li><strong>8th Amendment</strong> — prohibits cruel and unusual punishment, a protection frequently discussed today in the context of the death penalty debate.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 5b</span>\n    <p><strong>Applying the 4th Amendment.</strong> Police search a suspect's home without a warrant and without the suspect's consent, find incriminating evidence, and the prosecutor tries to introduce that evidence at trial. Explain how a court would likely handle this evidence.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">Absent a recognized exception (like consent, an emergency, or evidence in plain view), a warrantless search of a home without probable cause and a warrant violates the 4th Amendment. Under the <strong>exclusionary rule</strong> (Mapp v. Ohio), evidence obtained through an illegal search is generally inadmissible — a court would likely exclude the evidence from trial, even if it is genuinely incriminating, because the remedy for a 4th Amendment violation is barring the government from using the tainted evidence it obtained.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s36\"><span class=\"num\">3.6</span>The 14th Amendment and the Civil Rights Movement</h2>\n  <p>While civil liberties concern limits on government power over individuals generally, civil rights concern government's obligation to treat people EQUALLY — and the 14th Amendment's Equal Protection Clause is the central constitutional text underlying this entire area.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — The Emancipation Proclamation: Executive Power and the Roots of the Civil Rights Amendments</span>\n    <p><strong>The Emancipation Proclamation (1863)</strong>, issued by President Abraham Lincoln during the Civil War, declared that enslaved people in the states then in rebellion against the United States were free. Lincoln grounded this action in his power as <strong>commander-in-chief</strong> during wartime — a unilateral exercise of presidential emergency authority, not an act of ordinary civil-rights legislation passed by Congress. Because it rested on wartime military authority rather than a general power to regulate slavery, the Proclamation applied only within Confederate territory still in rebellion; it did not — and legally could not — free enslaved people in the border states that had remained loyal to the Union or in Confederate areas already back under Union military control. The Proclamation is best understood as a foundational first step in the constitutional lineage that runs through the 13th Amendment (which finally abolished slavery nationwide), the 14th Amendment's Equal Protection Clause, and the 15th Amendment, and ultimately toward the Civil Rights Movement material covered later in this section.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>The single most commonly missed fact about the Emancipation Proclamation: it did NOT immediately end slavery everywhere in the United States. It freed enslaved people only in states, or parts of states, then in rebellion against the Union — it did not apply to the border states (such as Kentucky, Maryland, Missouri, and Delaware) that had never seceded, nor to Confederate areas already back under Union control. Slavery was not abolished nationwide until the <strong>13th Amendment</strong> was ratified in 1865. Also keep the source of Lincoln's authority straight: the Proclamation rests on the president's <strong>wartime commander-in-chief power</strong>, not on ordinary legislative authority to regulate civil rights — a useful point of contrast with this section's later material on the 14th Amendment and civil rights legislation.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Brown v. Board of Education Overturned \"Separate but Equal\"</span>\n    <p><strong>Brown v. Board of Education (1954)</strong> ruled that racially segregated public schools violate the Equal Protection Clause of the 14th Amendment, explicitly overturning the \"separate but equal\" doctrine (from the earlier, non-required case Plessy v. Ferguson) as applied to public education. The Court reasoned that segregated educational facilities are inherently unequal, regardless of whether the physical facilities themselves were comparable — the very act of separation by race generated harmful inequality on its own. This ruling is widely regarded as a foundational catalyst for the broader Civil Rights Movement that followed.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — \"Letter from a Birmingham Jail\" Justifies Civil Disobedience</span>\n    <p>\"Letter from a Birmingham Jail,\" a required foundational document written by Martin Luther King Jr. in 1963, responds to criticism from fellow clergy who argued civil rights activists should wait patiently for change through the courts rather than engaging in direct action and civil disobedience. King argues that individuals have a moral responsibility to disobey UNJUST laws (which he distinguishes from just laws) openly and peacefully, and that waiting for gradual change ignores the urgent, lived reality of ongoing injustice. The letter is a foundational articulation of the moral and philosophical case for nonviolent civil disobedience in pursuit of civil rights.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>Brown v. Board of Education specifically addressed segregation in PUBLIC EDUCATION — its reasoning was later extended to other contexts by subsequent rulings and legislation, but the case's own holding is precise and shouldn't be described as having single-handedly ended all forms of racial segregation everywhere in one stroke. Similarly, \"Letter from a Birmingham Jail\" is a moral and philosophical argument, not a legal ruling — don't confuse it with a court case or attribute a legal holding to it.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 6</span>\n    <p><strong>Applying King's Distinction Between Just and Unjust Laws.</strong> Explain the reasoning King uses in \"Letter from a Birmingham Jail\" to justify breaking a law he considers unjust.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">King argues that an unjust law is one that degrades human personality or is imposed on a minority group that had no voice in creating it (such as segregation laws enacted by legislatures where Black citizens were systematically denied the right to vote). He argues individuals have a moral duty to disobey such unjust laws — but to do so OPENLY, peacefully, and while willingly accepting the legal penalty, as a way of demonstrating the deepest possible respect for the rule of law overall, even while refusing to comply with a specific unjust provision. This distinguishes his argument from lawlessness: King isn't arguing laws in general should be ignored, but that a specific category of laws — those that are fundamentally unjust — creates a moral obligation to resist, through nonviolent, consequence-accepting civil disobedience.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Explain what the Emancipation Proclamation actually did, and why it did not immediately end slavery everywhere.</li>\n      <li>State the holding of Brown v. Board of Education and explain its reasoning about inherent inequality.</li>\n      <li>Explain King's argument in \"Letter from a Birmingham Jail\" for civil disobedience against unjust laws.</li>\n      <li>Distinguish civil disobedience (a moral/political argument) from a legal ruling (a court's binding decision).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">Q</span><span>A student writes on the exam: \"Brown v. Board of Education legally required King's civil disobedience campaign, since the Court had already outlawed segregation.\" What's wrong with this statement?</span></div>\n  <div class=\"answer-key\"><details><summary>Check your answer</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Two errors stacked together. First, Brown v. Board (1954) addressed segregation specifically in public education — it didn't outlaw segregation everywhere all at once. Second, and more importantly, a court ruling doesn't \"require\" civil disobedience — King's argument in \"Letter from a Birmingham Jail\" is a separate moral and philosophical case for WHY individuals have a duty to resist unjust laws directly, not a legal consequence that flows automatically from a prior case. Keep the document (a persuasive argument) and the case (a binding legal ruling) analytically separate, even when they're historically connected.</div></details></div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s37\"><span class=\"num\">3.7</span>Balancing Liberty, Equality, and Order</h2>\n  <p>This final section pulls together a theme running through the entire unit: civil liberties and civil rights are rarely absolute — courts and government constantly have to balance individual rights against competing interests like public order, safety, and other individuals' rights.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — The Three Tiers of Equal Protection Scrutiny</span>\n    <p>Courts don't apply a single, uniform standard to every rights claim — some rights and some types of government classification receive more skeptical judicial scrutiny than others. Equal protection claims specifically use a three-tiered framework:</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <table class=\"formula-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Level of Scrutiny</th><th>Applies To</th><th>Government Must Show</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Strict scrutiny</td><td>Race, national origin, religion, and other fundamental rights</td><td>A <strong>compelling</strong> government interest, achieved through <strong>narrowly tailored</strong> means</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Intermediate scrutiny</td><td>Sex/gender classifications (e.g., <em>Craig v. Boren</em>, non-required but the standard example)</td><td>An <strong>important</strong> government interest, achieved through means <strong>substantially related</strong> to that interest</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Rational basis</td><td>Most other classifications, such as economic regulation or age</td><td>A <strong>legitimate</strong> government interest, achieved through means <strong>rationally related</strong> to that interest — the most deferential standard, and the easiest for government to satisfy</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <p>Restrictions on student speech are a separate matter, evaluated under Tinker's more specific \"substantial disruption\" standard rather than this three-tiered framework. Recognizing which specific standard or test applies to a given type of claim is often the key to correctly predicting how a court would likely rule.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 7b</span>\n    <p><strong>Applying Intermediate Scrutiny.</strong> A state law sets a different minimum drinking age for men than for women, with the state arguing this reduces drunk-driving rates among young men. Using the three-tiered scrutiny framework, evaluate how a court would most likely analyze this law.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">Because this law classifies people by <strong>sex</strong>, a court would apply <strong>intermediate scrutiny</strong> (not strict scrutiny, which is reserved for classifications like race) — requiring the state to show an <strong>important</strong> government interest achieved through means <strong>substantially related</strong> to that interest, following the reasoning of Craig v. Boren, the classic case applying this standard to a nearly identical sex-based drinking-age law. Reducing drunk driving is a legitimate and arguably important interest, but the state would need to show the sex-based age difference is substantially related to that goal (not simply loosely correlated) — a real evidentiary burden intermediate scrutiny imposes that rational basis review would not.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 7</span>\n    <p><strong>Synthesizing the Unit's Themes.</strong> A city passes an ordinance restricting a specific religious group's ability to gather for worship, citing public health concerns. Explain the competing interests at stake, and which required case's reasoning might inform how a court balances them.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">This scenario pits <strong>free exercise of religion</strong> against the government's interest in <strong>public order and safety</strong> (public health). A court would likely apply reasoning similar to Wisconsin v. Yoder's balancing approach — weighing the burden the ordinance places on the religious group's sincere religious practice against the strength and legitimacy of the government's stated interest, and asking whether the restriction is genuinely necessary to serve that interest or could be achieved through a less burdensome alternative. The outcome would likely depend heavily on the specific facts: how severe the public health concern genuinely is, how narrowly tailored the restriction is, and how substantially it burdens the religious practice in question.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Explain why courts apply different levels of scrutiny to different types of rights claims.</li>\n      <li>Identify which standard or test (strict scrutiny, intermediate scrutiny, rational basis, substantial disruption, clear and present danger, free exercise balancing) applies to a given scenario.</li>\n      <li>Distinguish the three tiers of equal protection scrutiny — strict, intermediate, and rational basis — including what interest and fit each requires.</li>\n      <li>Synthesize multiple required cases to analyze a novel scenario involving competing liberty and order interests.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"practice\">Unit 3 Practice Set</h2>\n  <p>Work through every problem before checking the answer key.</p>\n\n  <h3>Conceptual</h3>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">1</span><span>Distinguish civil liberties from civil rights, with an example of each.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">2</span><span>Explain why Gideon v. Wainwright and McDonald v. Chicago are both described as incorporation cases.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">3</span><span>Distinguish the Establishment Clause from the Free Exercise Clause.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">4</span><span>Explain the \"substantial disruption\" standard from Tinker v. Des Moines.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">5</span><span>Explain why prior restraint is treated as a particularly serious First Amendment concern.</span></div>\n\n  <h3>Application</h3>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">6</span><span>A public school leads students in a daily nondenominational prayer, with attendance technically optional. Which required case is most directly relevant, and what would it suggest about the practice's constitutionality?</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">7</span><span>A state requires all children to attend school through age 16, with no religious exemptions of any kind. A religious community argues this violates their beliefs. Which required case is most directly relevant?</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">8</span><span>A student is suspended for silently wearing a political pin to school, with no disruption to any class activity. Evaluate the suspension using Tinker v. Des Moines.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">9</span><span>Explain the original legal reasoning in Roe v. Wade, and identify the later case that overturned it.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">10</span><span>Explain how Brown v. Board of Education's reasoning about \"inherent inequality\" differed from simply comparing the physical quality of segregated facilities.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">11</span><span>According to \"Letter from a Birmingham Jail,\" what must someone do after breaking an unjust law, in order for their act to count as legitimate civil disobedience rather than simple lawbreaking?</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">12</span><span>A government policy classifies individuals by race. What level of judicial scrutiny would this policy likely receive?</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">13</span><span>Explain the key difference between what New York Times Co. v. United States decided about publishing classified information in advance, versus potential legal consequences after publication.</span></div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"margin-top:30px;\">Answer Key</h3>\n  <div class=\"answer-key\">\n    <details><summary>Question 1</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Civil liberties are protections FROM government overreach (e.g., freedom of speech). Civil rights are protections of equal treatment BY government (e.g., the right to vote regardless of race).</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 2</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Both cases used the 14th Amendment's Due Process Clause to apply a specific Bill of Rights protection to state governments — Gideon incorporated the 6th Amendment right to counsel; McDonald incorporated the 2nd Amendment right to bear arms — extending rights that originally applied only against the federal government.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 3</summary><div class=\"a-content\">The Establishment Clause prevents government from establishing, promoting, or favoring a religion. The Free Exercise Clause prevents government from unduly restricting an individual's ability to practice their own religion.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 4</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Schools may restrict student speech only when it would cause a substantial disruption to the educational environment or infringe on the rights of others — mere disagreement or discomfort with the speech's content is not sufficient justification for restricting it.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 5</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Prior restraint blocks speech or publication before it ever occurs, which courts view as an especially severe form of censorship compared to addressing speech after the fact — it prevents information or ideas from ever reaching the public at all, rather than allowing for consequences after publication.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 6</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Engel v. Vitale is most directly relevant — it ruled that school-sponsored prayer violates the Establishment Clause even when nondenominational and technically voluntary, since government's official endorsement of the practice is itself the constitutional problem. This practice would likely be found unconstitutional.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 7</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Wisconsin v. Yoder is most directly relevant — it ruled that a state's compulsory education requirement could not override the Amish community's free exercise rights when the burden on their religious practice was substantial and the state's interest was insufficient to override it in that specific context.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 8</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Under Tinker v. Des Moines, this suspension is likely unconstitutional — silent, non-disruptive symbolic political expression is protected student speech, and the absence of any actual disruption to school activities means the \"substantial disruption\" standard is not met.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 9</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Roe v. Wade (1973) held that the right to privacy, grounded in the 14th Amendment's Due Process Clause, extended to a woman's decision to have an abortion, subject to a framework balancing that right against state interests. Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization (2022) later overturned Roe v. Wade, ruling there is no constitutional right to abortion.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 10</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Brown v. Board of Education held that segregated schools were inherently unequal simply because of the act of separation itself, regardless of whether the physical facilities were comparable in quality — rejecting the \"separate but equal\" framework that had focused only on comparing the tangible quality of segregated facilities.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 11</summary><div class=\"a-content\">According to King, someone practicing legitimate civil disobedience must break the unjust law openly, peacefully (nonviolently), and willingly accept the legal penalty for doing so — this demonstrates respect for the rule of law overall, distinguishing principled civil disobedience from ordinary lawbreaking.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 12</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Strict scrutiny — the most demanding level of judicial review, requiring the government to prove a compelling interest and that the policy is narrowly tailored to achieve it. Racial classifications by government are a classic trigger for strict scrutiny.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 13</summary><div class=\"a-content\">New York Times Co. v. United States specifically ruled the government could not block publication IN ADVANCE (prior restraint), since it failed to meet the heavy burden required to justify that. The ruling did not fully resolve whether the newspapers could face separate legal consequences after publication — those are distinct legal questions.</div></details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"footer-nav\">\n    Next up: head to the <strong>Unit 3 QBank</strong> to drill these ideas, then move on to <strong>Unit 4 — American Political Ideologies and Beliefs</strong>.\n  </div>\n\n</div>\n",
  "4": "<div class=\"hero\">\n  <div class=\"eyebrow\">Peters Family Homeschool · AP U.S. Government &amp; Politics</div>\n  <h1>American Political Ideologies and Beliefs</h1>\n  <p class=\"sub\">Where does a political opinion actually come from? This unit is the course's turn inward — away from institutions and toward the people those institutions are supposed to represent. It's also where \"liberal\" and \"conservative\" stop being casual labels and become precise, testable descriptions of consistent policy positions.</p>\n  <div class=\"hero-pills\">\n    <span class=\"pill\">Exam weight 10–15%</span>\n    <span class=\"pill\">6 topics</span>\n    <span class=\"pill\">65 practice questions</span>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"toc\">\n  <div class=\"toc-label\">Jump to a section</div>\n  <ol>\n    <li><a href=\"#s41\">4.1 American Political Culture and Core Values</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s42\">4.2 Political Socialization</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s43\">4.3 Public Opinion Polling</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s44\">4.4 Liberal and Conservative Ideology</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s45\">4.5 Ideology and Party Platforms</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s46\">4.6 Ideology in Government Policy</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#practice\">Practice Set</a></li>\n  </ol>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"wrap\">\n\n  <div class=\"skillbox\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label skill\">Master This First — Ideology Predicts Policy Positions, Not Just Vibes</span>\n    <p>The biggest mistake students make in this unit is treating \"liberal\" and \"conservative\" as vague cultural labels rather than as reasonably consistent, predictable clusters of policy positions. If you know someone favors a larger role for government in regulating the economy and providing a social safety net, you can reasonably predict several OTHER positions they're likely to hold, even on topics you haven't discussed with them. This unit rewards being able to reason FROM a stated ideology TO a predicted policy position, and vice versa — that's the actual skill being tested, far more than memorizing a list of adjectives.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s41\"><span class=\"num\">4.1</span>American Political Culture and Core Values</h2>\n  <p>Despite deep and genuine political disagreement, most Americans share a set of core values that make up the country's political culture — the disagreements this course spends so much time on are usually about how to BALANCE these shared values against each other, not whether the values themselves matter.</p>\n\n  <table class=\"formula-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Core Value</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Liberty</td><td>Freedom from excessive government interference in individual life and choices</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Equality</td><td>Equal treatment under the law and, for some, equal opportunity in economic and social life</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Individualism</td><td>Emphasis on personal responsibility and self-reliance over collective or government provision</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Self-Government</td><td>The belief that political authority should rest on the consent and participation of the governed</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Free Enterprise</td><td>A cultural preference for capitalism and private economic ownership, with government's economic role expected to be limited — related to, but more economically specific than, general \"liberty\"</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Shared Values, Genuine Disagreement About Tradeoffs</span>\n    <p>Almost nobody in American politics argues against liberty or equality as concepts — the actual disagreement is about how to weigh them against each other when they pull in different directions. A policy that redistributes income to reduce economic inequality may be seen as advancing equality while constraining individual economic liberty (through taxation); a policy that minimizes government regulation may be seen as advancing liberty while tolerating greater inequality of outcome. Recognizing that a policy debate is really a disagreement about HOW TO BALANCE shared values — not a disagreement about which values matter at all — is a genuinely useful lens for analyzing almost any described policy dispute on this exam.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>Don't assume that shared core values mean Americans agree on how those values should be applied in practice. Two people can both deeply value \"equality\" and reach opposite conclusions about a specific policy — one focusing on equality of OPPORTUNITY (equal starting conditions, minimal government interference), the other on equality of OUTCOME (more equal results, often requiring more active government intervention). The exam frequently tests whether you can identify which specific value-tension a described scenario reflects, not just whether you can name the values themselves.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 1</span>\n    <p><strong>Identifying a Value Tradeoff.</strong> A proposed policy would require all businesses to pay a minimum wage well above the current market rate. Explain the core value tradeoff at stake in this policy debate.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">Supporters would likely frame this policy as advancing <strong>equality</strong> — ensuring low-wage workers receive a more equitable share of economic gains and reducing income inequality. Opponents would likely frame the same policy as constraining <strong>liberty</strong> — specifically, businesses' economic freedom to set wages through voluntary agreement with employees, without government mandate. Both sides can genuinely value both liberty and equality; the disagreement is about which value should take priority in this specific tradeoff, and how severely the policy affects each.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Identify the core American political values — liberty, equality, individualism, self-government, and free enterprise — and describe each briefly.</li>\n      <li>Explain why political disagreement is often about balancing shared values, not disagreement about the values themselves.</li>\n      <li>Distinguish equality of opportunity from equality of outcome as two different applications of the same core value.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s41b\">Foundational Document: <em>The Wealth of Nations</em> (Adam Smith)</h2>\n  <p>One of the Foundational Documents for this course, Adam Smith's <em>The Wealth of Nations</em> (1776), is the canonical source AP Gov cites for <strong>free enterprise</strong> as a core American political value — it belongs here in 4.1, not because it's an economics textbook, but because it's the evidentiary basis for one of the five core values in the table above.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — The Core Argument: Self-Interest, Competition, and the \"Invisible Hand\"</span>\n    <p>Smith's central argument is that individuals pursuing their own self-interest in a competitive market, largely free of centralized direction, tend to produce outcomes that benefit society broadly — as if guided by an \"invisible hand.\" A baker doesn't produce bread out of charity; they produce it to earn a living, and competition among many such self-interested producers keeps prices reasonable and quality reasonably high without anyone centrally planning the outcome. From this, Smith draws a governing implication: government's proper economic role is generally limited — protecting property rights and enforcing contracts so markets can function — rather than directing production, setting prices, or otherwise managing the economy from the center.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>Don't treat <em>The Wealth of Nations</em> as just \"an economics book\" that happens to appear in a government course. For AP Gov purposes, what matters is narrower and more specific: it's the document the course points to as the ORIGIN of free enterprise as a shared American political value, alongside liberty, equality, individualism, and self-government. A question testing this document is testing whether you can connect Smith's argument to that specific role — not whether you can explain supply and demand in general.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 1b</span>\n    <p><strong>Connecting a Foundational Document to a Core Value.</strong> Explain how Adam Smith's <em>The Wealth of Nations</em> supports the idea that free enterprise is a core American political value, and identify where this value reappears later in this unit.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">Smith argues that individuals acting in their own self-interest, within a competitive market and with minimal government direction, tend to produce broadly beneficial economic outcomes (the \"invisible hand\") — and that government's economic role should generally be limited to protecting property rights and enforcing contracts rather than directing the economy. This argument supplies the intellectual foundation for free enterprise as a core American value: a cultural preference for capitalism and private ownership, with limited government economic involvement. This same value resurfaces later in the unit (4.4–4.6) as the economic core of <strong>conservative</strong> ideology — favoring less government regulation and greater reliance on free markets — making Smith's argument one pole of the liberal/conservative divide on economic policy covered there.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Explain Smith's core argument: self-interest plus competition, with limited government, tends to produce broadly beneficial economic outcomes (the \"invisible hand\").</li>\n      <li>Explain why this document counts as a Foundational Document for AP Gov specifically — as the source for free enterprise as a core value — not simply because it discusses economics.</li>\n      <li>Connect this value forward to conservative economic ideology (less regulation, greater reliance on markets) covered later in this unit.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s42\"><span class=\"num\">4.2</span>Political Socialization</h2>\n  <p>Political socialization is the process by which individuals develop their political beliefs and values over the course of their lives — and it happens through a fairly predictable set of \"agents,\" each with a different degree of influence at different life stages.</p>\n\n  <table class=\"formula-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Agent of Socialization</th><th>Typical Influence</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Family</td><td>Generally the strongest and earliest influence, especially on party identification</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>School</td><td>Formal civic education; exposure to democratic norms and processes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Religion</td><td>Shapes views on specific social and moral policy issues</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Peers</td><td>Growing influence during adolescence and young adulthood</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Media</td><td>Ongoing influence throughout life; shapes which issues seem salient</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Major life/historical events</td><td>Can shift an entire generation's political outlook (a \"generational effect\")</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Family Is the Single Strongest Predictor, Especially for Party ID</span>\n    <p>Of all the agents of socialization, family consistently shows the strongest and most durable influence on political beliefs, particularly on party identification — children raised in strongly partisan households are considerably more likely to adopt a similar party affiliation as adults than would be expected by chance. This doesn't mean family influence is absolute or unchangeable (other agents, and life experience, can and do shift people's views over time), but it's the single most reliable predictor among the standard agents of socialization covered in this course.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>Don't treat political socialization as a one-time event that happens only in childhood. While early-life influences (especially family) are particularly strong and durable, socialization is an ONGOING, lifelong process — major historical events, changing life circumstances (marriage, homeownership, parenthood), and evolving media consumption habits can all continue to shape political beliefs well into adulthood.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 2</span>\n    <p><strong>Identifying an Agent of Socialization.</strong> A young adult who grew up in a politically moderate household becomes considerably more politically engaged and shifts their views after spending several years working alongside politically active colleagues. Identify the primary agent of socialization at work.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">This scenario primarily illustrates the influence of <strong>peers</strong> (in this case, colleagues functioning in a peer-like social role) as an agent of political socialization. While family shaped this person's initial, moderate political starting point, their sustained social interaction with politically engaged colleagues in adulthood illustrates how socialization continues well beyond childhood, and how peer influence can meaningfully shift someone's views even after their family-shaped starting point was already established.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>List the major agents of political socialization and describe each one's typical influence.</li>\n      <li>Explain why family is generally considered the strongest single influence on party identification.</li>\n      <li>Explain why political socialization continues throughout adulthood rather than ending after childhood.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s43\"><span class=\"num\">4.3</span>Public Opinion Polling</h2>\n  <p>Polls are how political scientists, campaigns, and the media attempt to measure public opinion at scale — but a poll is only as trustworthy as the METHODOLOGY behind it, and this section is specifically about evaluating that methodology critically.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Random Sampling Is What Makes a Poll Generalizable</span>\n    <p>A poll's value depends heavily on whether its sample was drawn through <strong>random sampling</strong> — a method giving every member of the target population a roughly equal chance of being selected. A poll based on a non-random sample (say, only people who happened to visit a particular website, or callers who chose to respond to a call-in survey) cannot be reliably generalized to the broader population, no matter how many people respond, since the sample itself may be systematically unrepresentative in ways that are hard to detect or correct for.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <table class=\"formula-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>What It Means</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Sample size</td><td>The number of people surveyed; larger, well-drawn samples generally produce more precise estimates</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Sampling error</td><td>The natural, unavoidable gap between a randomly drawn sample's result and the true population value, simply because a sample (not the full population) was surveyed; the margin of error is the poll's numeric estimate of how large this gap is likely to be, usually at a 95% confidence level</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Margin of error</td><td>A measure of the poll's likely precision — a smaller margin means the reported result is more likely close to the true population value</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Question wording effects</td><td>How a question is phrased can significantly shift responses, even when asking about the same underlying issue</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Push poll</td><td>A poll designed to shape opinion under the guise of measuring it, often using biased or misleading question wording</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Exit poll</td><td>A survey of voters conducted as they leave polling places on election day; useful for early projections and demographic breakdowns of voting patterns, but subject to its own biases (e.g., systematically missing mail and early voters, and depending on which voters are willing to stop and respond)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Tracking poll</td><td>A poll repeated at regular intervals (e.g., daily or weekly) using a consistent methodology, designed to measure movement or momentum in opinion over time rather than a single point-in-time estimate</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>A large sample size does NOT compensate for a poorly, non-randomly drawn sample. A poll of one million self-selected online respondents can be far less reliable than a poll of one thousand carefully, randomly selected respondents — sample SIZE and sample QUALITY (how it was drawn) are two entirely separate issues, and a large but biased sample is still a biased sample, regardless of how impressively large the number looks.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 3</span>\n    <p><strong>Evaluating a Poll's Methodology.</strong> A news website posts an online poll asking visitors to vote on a political question, receiving 50,000 responses. Explain why this poll's results should be treated with significant skepticism, despite the large number of responses.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">The core problem is that this poll relies on a <strong>self-selected sample</strong>, not a random one — only people who happened to visit that specific website AND chose to participate are represented, and those two facts alone make the sample systematically unrepresentative of the broader population (skewing, for instance, toward that website's typical readership and toward people motivated enough to actively click and respond). The large number of responses (50,000) does nothing to fix this underlying sampling problem — a much smaller, genuinely random sample would actually produce more reliable, generalizable results than this large but non-random one.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Explain why random sampling is essential to a poll's reliability and generalizability.</li>\n      <li>Distinguish sample size from sample quality, and explain why a large sample cannot fix a non-random sampling method.</li>\n      <li>Identify a push poll and explain how biased question wording can distort measured public opinion.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s43b\">Evaluating and Using Public Opinion Data</h2>\n  <p>Beyond how a poll is properly constructed (covered above), a distinct and equally testable skill is evaluating how sound polling data is actually USED — and sometimes misused or overstated — in political discourse.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Statistical Validity Is Not the Same as Being Right About the Future</span>\n    <p>A properly random, correctly reported poll accurately describes public opinion <em>at the moment it was taken, among the people it successfully reached</em>. It does not guarantee that opinion will hold steady, that the people who said they'd act (e.g., vote) actually will, or that non-respondents share the same views as respondents. \"Sound methodology\" and \"correct prediction\" are different claims — a well-conducted poll can still be widely off from a later outcome for reasons that have nothing to do with sampling error, such as genuine opinion shifts between the poll date and the event, flawed \"likely voter\" turnout models, non-response bias, or social-desirability effects on sensitive questions.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Public Opinion Shapes Policy, But Unevenly and Imperfectly</span>\n    <p>Elected officials act as intermediaries between opinion and policy — they interpret, filter, and sometimes shape opinion rather than simply obeying it. Officials tend to be most responsive to public opinion on high-salience issues the public is known to track and vote on, and least responsive on low-salience or technical issues. Media coverage also shapes which issues seem important enough for the public (and officials) to form opinions about in the first place (<strong>agenda-setting</strong>), before any purely methodological questions about a poll's construction even come into play.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 3b</span>\n    <p><strong>Distinguishing Validity from Predictive Accuracy.</strong> A properly random, well-conducted poll taken two weeks before an election shows Candidate A leading by 6 points. Candidate A goes on to lose narrowly. A commentator claims this proves the poll was \"unscientific.\" Evaluate this claim.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">The commentator's claim is not well supported. A properly random sample with correctly reported methodology can be entirely statistically sound and still fail to predict the eventual outcome, because opinion can genuinely shift in the two weeks between the poll and the election, \"likely voter\" screens can misjudge actual turnout, or non-response bias can leave certain voters underrepresented even in an otherwise random sample. \"Statistically valid\" and \"correctly predicted the outcome\" are different claims — a missed prediction does not, by itself, mean the poll's methodology was flawed.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Explain why a methodologically sound poll can still fail to predict a later real-world outcome.</li>\n      <li>Explain how elected officials act as filters between public opinion and actual policy, and how salience affects their responsiveness.</li>\n      <li>Explain agenda-setting's role in shaping which issues the public forms opinions about in the first place.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s44\"><span class=\"num\">4.4</span>Liberal and Conservative Ideology</h2>\n  <p>This section gets to the heart of the unit: what does it actually, precisely mean to identify as liberal or conservative, in terms of consistent, predictable policy positions rather than vague cultural labels?</p>\n\n  <table class=\"formula-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Issue Area</th><th>Typical Liberal Position</th><th>Typical Conservative Position</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Role of government in the economy</td><td>More government regulation and intervention to address market failures and inequality</td><td>Less government regulation; greater reliance on free markets</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Social safety net</td><td>Larger, more expansive government-funded programs</td><td>Smaller government role; greater emphasis on individual/private responsibility</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Social issues</td><td>Generally more supportive of expanding social change and individual autonomy in personal choices</td><td>Generally more supportive of traditional social norms and values</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Government spending/taxation</td><td>Generally more supportive of higher taxation to fund government programs</td><td>Generally more supportive of lower taxation and reduced government spending</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Ideology Isn't Always a Perfectly Consistent Package</span>\n    <p>While liberal and conservative positions tend to cluster together fairly reliably, real individuals don't always fit neatly into one consistent ideological box. A <strong>libertarian</strong>, for instance, typically favors minimal government intervention in BOTH the economy AND personal/social life — combining a conservative-leaning economic position with a liberal-leaning position on personal autonomy in social issues. A <strong>populist</strong> generally distrusts concentrated economic and political power and favors government protecting ordinary workers and consumers from that power — but which specific policies follow from this varies by tradition. Historically, agrarian and New Deal-era populism favored MORE regulation of large corporations (railroads, banks, trusts) alongside an expansive safety net. Some strands of more recent populism instead favor LESS domestic business regulation combined with trade protectionism (tariffs on foreign competitors) and continued strong support for existing entitlement programs. What unites these different combinations is suspicion of concentrated elite power, not a single fixed position on the size of government. Don't assume every individual's views map perfectly onto a single, internally consistent liberal-or-conservative package.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>\"Liberal\" and \"conservative,\" as used in American politics, are not universal, fixed categories — their specific meaning is tied to the current American political context, and even within that context, the SPECIFIC POLICY POSITIONS associated with each label can shift somewhat over time. Focus on the general PATTERN this course tests (attitudes toward the size/role of government, and toward social change vs. tradition) rather than treating any single specific policy position as permanently, unchangeably \"the\" liberal or conservative position for all time.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 4</span>\n    <p><strong>Predicting a Position from Ideology.</strong> A politician describes themselves as strongly favoring free-market solutions and minimal government regulation of business, while also supporting significant government funding for a broad social safety net. Evaluate whether this combination of positions is ideologically consistent.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">This combination is somewhat ideologically INCONSISTENT with a standard, single-package liberal or conservative profile — favoring minimal regulation of business is a typically conservative economic position, while favoring a large, well-funded social safety net is a typically liberal economic position. This kind of mixed profile suggests either a more nuanced, cross-cutting set of views (not uncommon among real individuals) or potentially a welfare-preserving strand of populism (see 4.4) that favors continued government support for existing safety-net programs while opposing new regulatory burdens on business — distinct from the pro-regulation agrarian/New Deal populist tradition also discussed there. Either way, this illustrates why not every set of political views maps cleanly onto the standard two-category framework.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Describe the typical liberal and conservative positions across several major issue areas.</li>\n      <li>Explain why libertarian and populist positions don't fit neatly into the standard liberal/conservative framework.</li>\n      <li>Predict a likely policy position given a described ideological orientation, and vice versa.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s45\"><span class=\"num\">4.5</span>Ideology and Party Platforms</h2>\n  <p>American political parties generally organize their official platforms around one of the two dominant ideological clusters — though the exact fit between \"party\" and \"ideology\" is looser and more complicated in practice than it might initially appear.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Parties Are Broad Coalitions, Not Perfectly Uniform Blocs</span>\n    <p>While the Democratic Party's platform generally aligns more closely with liberal positions and the Republican Party's platform generally aligns more closely with conservative positions, neither party is perfectly ideologically uniform. Both parties contain meaningful internal diversity — factions that lean more toward the political center, and factions that lean more toward each party's more ideologically extreme wing. This internal diversity matters for understanding real legislative behavior, since it means party affiliation alone doesn't perfectly predict every individual member's position on every single issue.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 5</span>\n    <p><strong>Applying Party-Ideology Alignment.</strong> A candidate's official platform emphasizes expanding government-funded healthcare access and increasing taxes on high earners to fund it. Which major party's platform does this most closely resemble, and why?</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">This platform most closely resembles the <strong>Democratic Party's</strong> general platform, which typically favors expanding government-funded social programs and using progressive taxation to fund them — both positions align with the broader liberal ideological cluster around a larger, more active government role in the economy and social welfare. It's worth noting this doesn't mean every single Democratic officeholder holds an identical position — internal party diversity means individual variation exists even within a generally aligned platform.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Explain the general ideological alignment of the two major American political parties.</li>\n      <li>Explain why parties should be understood as broad coalitions rather than perfectly uniform ideological blocs.</li>\n      <li>Match a described policy platform to the party it most closely resembles, and explain the underlying ideological reasoning.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s46\"><span class=\"num\">4.6</span>Ideology in Government Policy</h2>\n  <p>This final section connects the unit's abstract ideological framework directly to concrete policy outcomes — the actual test of whether you can move fluently between \"ideology\" as a concept and specific, real policy positions.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Ideology Shapes Both WHAT Policy Is Chosen and HOW It's Implemented</span>\n    <p>Ideological differences show up not just in WHETHER government should act on a given problem, but in HOW it should act if it does. Two policymakers might both agree that a problem (say, poverty) deserves a government response, while disagreeing sharply about the best TYPE of response — one favoring direct cash assistance programs, the other favoring tax incentives designed to encourage private-sector job creation. Recognizing this distinction (agreement that government should act, disagreement about how) is a more sophisticated and often more accurate way to characterize real policy debates than assuming disagreement is always about whether government should be involved at all.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 6</span>\n    <p><strong>Ideology and Policy Design.</strong> Two lawmakers both want to reduce a state's carbon emissions. One proposes a strict government regulation setting maximum emission limits for each company. The other proposes a market-based tax credit system rewarding companies that reduce emissions voluntarily. Explain how this reflects differing ideological approaches to the same underlying policy goal.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">Both lawmakers share the same GOAL (reduced emissions), but their proposed METHODS reflect differing ideological approaches to government's proper role. The strict regulatory approach reflects a more typically liberal preference for direct government intervention and mandated compliance. The market-based tax credit approach reflects a more typically conservative preference for using market incentives and private choice to achieve a public goal, minimizing direct government mandate. This example illustrates how ideological differences often concern the appropriate MEANS to a shared end, not just disagreement about the end itself.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Explain how ideology shapes not just whether government acts, but how it acts.</li>\n      <li>Distinguish agreement on policy goals from disagreement on policy methods.</li>\n      <li>Analyze a described policy proposal for the ideological assumptions embedded in its design.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"practice\">Unit 4 Practice Set</h2>\n  <p>Work through every problem before checking the answer key.</p>\n\n  <h3>Conceptual</h3>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">1</span><span>Explain the difference between equality of opportunity and equality of outcome, and give a policy example associated with each.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">2</span><span>Identify the agent of political socialization generally considered the strongest and earliest influence on party identification.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">3</span><span>Explain why a poll's sample size cannot compensate for a non-random sampling method.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">4</span><span>Describe the typically liberal and typically conservative positions on the appropriate size and role of government in the economy.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">5</span><span>Explain why a libertarian's combination of views doesn't fit neatly into the standard liberal/conservative framework.</span></div>\n\n  <h3>Application</h3>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">6</span><span>A campaign uses a survey with leading, biased question wording designed to shift respondents' opinions while appearing to measure them. Identify this technique.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">7</span><span>A voter strongly favors both lower taxes on businesses and increased government funding for public healthcare. Evaluate the ideological consistency of this combination.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">8</span><span>Two lawmakers agree that unemployment is a serious problem but propose very different government responses. Explain how this reflects ideological differences about policy methods rather than policy goals.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">9</span><span>A person's political views shift substantially after a major national economic crisis affects their community. Which broader socialization concept does this illustrate?</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">10</span><span>Explain why party platforms should be understood as broad coalitions rather than perfectly uniform ideological positions.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">11</span><span>A national poll of 1,200 randomly selected adults reports a candidate's approval rating with a stated margin of error of 3 points. Explain what the margin of error communicates about the poll's precision.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">12</span><span>Identify the core American value most directly at stake in a policy debate over whether wealthy individuals should pay a higher tax rate than lower earners.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">13</span><span>A candidate proposes achieving a policy goal through market-based incentives rather than direct government mandates. Which ideological tradition does this approach typically reflect?</span></div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"margin-top:30px;\">Answer Key</h3>\n  <div class=\"answer-key\">\n    <details><summary>Question 1</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Equality of opportunity means ensuring equal starting conditions (e.g., equal access to education), generally with minimal government interference beyond that. Equality of outcome means working toward more equal actual results (e.g., income redistribution), often requiring more active government intervention.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 2</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Family — generally the strongest and earliest influence on political beliefs, especially party identification, among the standard agents of socialization.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 3</summary><div class=\"a-content\">A large sample size only helps if the sample was drawn randomly in the first place. A large but non-randomly (e.g., self-selected) drawn sample remains systematically unrepresentative of the broader population, regardless of how many people it includes.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 4</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Liberals typically favor a larger government role, including more regulation and intervention to address market failures and inequality. Conservatives typically favor a smaller government role and greater reliance on free markets.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 5</summary><div class=\"a-content\">A libertarian typically favors minimal government intervention in BOTH the economy (a conservative-leaning position) and personal/social life (a liberal-leaning position), combining elements from both standard ideological clusters rather than fitting into just one.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 6</summary><div class=\"a-content\">A push poll — a survey designed to shape public opinion under the guise of measuring it, typically through biased or misleading question wording.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 7</summary><div class=\"a-content\">This combination is somewhat ideologically inconsistent with a single standard package — lower business taxes reflects a typically conservative economic position, while expanded government healthcare funding reflects a typically liberal economic position — illustrating that real individuals don't always hold perfectly consistent ideological bundles.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 8</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Both lawmakers share the same GOAL (addressing unemployment) but differ on the appropriate METHOD — reflecting differing ideological assumptions about the best way for government to intervene (or not) in the economy, rather than a disagreement about whether the problem deserves attention at all.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 9</summary><div class=\"a-content\">This illustrates how major historical/life events can shape political socialization and beliefs well into adulthood — socialization is an ongoing, lifelong process, not something that concludes after childhood.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 10</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Because both major parties contain meaningful internal ideological diversity — factions leaning more centrist and factions leaning more toward each party's ideological extreme — meaning party affiliation alone doesn't perfectly predict every individual member's position on every issue.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 11</summary><div class=\"a-content\">The margin of error indicates the poll's likely precision — the reported approval rating is likely within about 3 percentage points of the true population value, assuming the sample was properly, randomly drawn.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 12</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Equality (specifically, a version of the liberty-versus-equality tradeoff) — supporters would frame higher rates on wealthy individuals as advancing equality, while opponents would frame it as constraining individual economic liberty.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 13</summary><div class=\"a-content\">This reflects a typically conservative ideological approach, favoring market-based incentives and private choice over direct government mandate as the preferred means of achieving a policy goal.</div></details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"footer-nav\">\n    Next up: head to the <strong>Unit 4 QBank</strong> to drill these ideas, then move on to <strong>Unit 5 — Political Participation</strong>, the final unit of the course.\n  </div>\n\n</div>\n",
  "5": "<div class=\"hero\">\n  <div class=\"eyebrow\">Peters Family Homeschool · AP U.S. Government &amp; Politics</div>\n  <h1>Political Participation</h1>\n  <p class=\"sub\">The final unit of the course, and the one that finally answers the question every prior unit has been quietly building toward: how do ordinary people actually plug into this whole system? Voting, parties, interest groups, campaigns, and media are the linkage institutions connecting the governed to the governors — and this unit is where the last required Supreme Court case, Citizens United, finally makes its appearance.</p>\n  <div class=\"hero-pills\">\n    <span class=\"pill\">Exam weight 20–27%</span>\n    <span class=\"pill\">8 topics</span>\n    <span class=\"pill\">65 practice questions</span>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"toc\">\n  <div class=\"toc-label\">Jump to a section</div>\n  <ol>\n    <li><a href=\"#s51\">5.1 Voting Rights and Voting Behavior</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s52\">5.2 Voter Turnout and Its Determinants</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s53\">5.3 Political Parties — Structure and Function</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s54\">5.4 Party Realignment and Change Over Time</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s55\">5.5 Third Parties and Their Role</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s56\">5.6 Interest Groups and Policy Influence</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s57\">5.7 Campaigns, Elections, and Campaign Finance</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#s58\">5.8 The Media's Role in Politics</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#practice\">Practice Set</a></li>\n  </ol>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"wrap\">\n\n  <div class=\"skillbox\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label skill\">Master This First — \"Linkage Institutions\" Is the Unifying Concept</span>\n    <p>Every topic in this unit — elections, parties, interest groups, media — is a <strong>linkage institution</strong>: a channel connecting ordinary citizens' preferences to the decisions government actually makes. None of these institutions are explicitly detailed in the Constitution's text (parties aren't mentioned at all; the modern media landscape obviously didn't exist in 1787), yet all of them have become essential, functional parts of how American democracy actually operates in practice. Whenever you're unsure how a specific topic in this unit fits into the bigger picture, ask: \"how does this connect citizen preferences to government action?\" That question is the throughline for the entire unit.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s51\"><span class=\"num\">5.1</span>Voting Rights and Voting Behavior</h2>\n  <p>The right to vote in America wasn't granted all at once — it expanded gradually, through a sequence of constitutional amendments and legislation, each removing a specific barrier to participation.</p>\n\n  <table class=\"formula-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Amendment/Law</th><th>Expansion of Voting Rights</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>15th Amendment (1870)</td><td>Prohibited denying the vote based on race</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>19th Amendment (1920)</td><td>Prohibited denying the vote based on sex</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>24th Amendment (1964)</td><td>Banned poll taxes in federal elections</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Voting Rights Act (1965)</td><td>Banned literacy tests and other discriminatory practices; provided federal enforcement</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>26th Amendment (1971)</td><td>Lowered the voting age to 18</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Formal Legal Right to Vote Isn't the Same as Actually Voting</span>\n    <p>Once a barrier to voting is legally removed, that doesn't automatically mean everyone newly eligible actually turns out to vote — the legal RIGHT and the practical BEHAVIOR are two separate things, connected but distinct. This distinction matters for the rest of the unit: Section 5.2 is specifically about why people who legally CAN vote often don't, which is a genuinely different question from who is legally entitled to vote in the first place.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <table class=\"formula-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Model of Voting Behavior</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Rational choice voting</td><td>Voting for the candidate whose policy positions most closely match the voter's own preferences</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Retrospective voting</td><td>Voting based on evaluation of an incumbent's PAST performance in office</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Prospective voting</td><td>Voting based on a candidate's promises about FUTURE policy</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Party-line voting</td><td>Voting based primarily on party affiliation, regardless of the specific candidate or issue</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 1</span>\n    <p><strong>Identifying a Voting Model.</strong> A voter decides to vote against an incumbent governor primarily because the state's unemployment rate rose significantly during that governor's term. Which model of voting behavior does this illustrate?</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">This is <strong>retrospective voting</strong> — the voter is evaluating the incumbent's PAST performance in office (the unemployment increase during their term) rather than comparing specific future policy proposals (which would be prospective voting) or simply voting along party lines regardless of performance.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Sequence the major constitutional amendments and legislation expanding voting rights.</li>\n      <li>Explain why having a legal right to vote is distinct from actually exercising that right.</li>\n      <li>Distinguish a de jure barrier to voting from a de facto barrier, with an example of each.</li>\n      <li>Distinguish rational choice, retrospective, prospective, and party-line voting, and identify which applies to a described scenario.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — De Jure vs. De Facto Barriers to Voting</span>\n    <p>A <strong>de jure</strong> barrier explicitly and legally denies a group the right to vote (e.g., laws that once explicitly barred women or Black Americans from voting before the 19th and 15th Amendments). A <strong>de facto</strong> barrier is facially neutral — it does not legally single out any group — but functions in practice to suppress turnout disproportionately among specific groups (e.g., a literacy test applied unevenly, a poll tax that disproportionately burdens lower-income voters, or a strict voter-ID law in an area where certain groups are less likely to hold qualifying ID). The Voting Rights Act (1965) specifically targeted de facto discriminatory practices that had survived despite the 15th Amendment's de jure guarantee.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s52\"><span class=\"num\">5.2</span>Voter Turnout and Its Determinants</h2>\n  <p>Even with expansive legal voting rights, American voter turnout is famously lower than in many other established democracies — and political scientists have identified a fairly consistent set of factors that predict who actually votes and who doesn't.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Socioeconomic Status Is One of the Strongest Predictors of Turnout</span>\n    <p>Higher levels of education and income are consistently among the strongest predictors of voter turnout — individuals with more education and higher income vote at meaningfully higher rates, on average, than those with less. Age is another strong predictor: older citizens generally turn out at higher rates than younger citizens. Beyond these demographic factors, RACE-NEUTRAL structural and administrative factors also matter enormously: registration requirements and deadlines, the ease or difficulty of the voting process itself, and how competitive a given race is (closer races tend to drive higher turnout, since individual votes feel more consequential).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>Don't treat \"low turnout\" as a single, uniform phenomenon with one cause — it results from a genuine MIX of factors operating simultaneously: individual-level factors (socioeconomic status, age, interest in politics), structural factors (registration deadlines and requirements, election timing, voting method availability), and contextual factors (how competitive and salient a particular election feels to voters). A strong exam answer usually identifies the SPECIFIC factor(s) most relevant to a described scenario, rather than a vague, generic statement that \"people don't vote.\"</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 2</span>\n    <p><strong>Analyzing a Turnout Factor.</strong> A state moves its voter registration deadline from 30 days before an election to same-day registration at the polls. Predict the likely effect on turnout, and explain why.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">This change would likely <strong>increase turnout</strong>. Registration deadlines are a structural/administrative barrier to voting — requiring registration weeks in advance means some otherwise-eligible voters simply forget or miss the deadline before they've fully engaged with the upcoming election. Same-day registration removes this specific administrative barrier, allowing eligible citizens to register and vote in a single visit, which research consistently shows increases turnout by reducing the practical friction involved in participating.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Identify the major individual-level and structural factors that predict voter turnout.</li>\n      <li>Explain why socioeconomic status and age are among the strongest predictors of turnout.</li>\n      <li>Predict how a specific structural change (like registration rules) would likely affect turnout, and explain why.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s53\"><span class=\"num\">5.3</span>Political Parties — Structure and Function</h2>\n  <p>Political parties, though never explicitly mentioned in the Constitution, have become one of the most essential linkage institutions in American politics — performing a set of core functions no other single institution replicates in quite the same way.</p>\n\n  <table class=\"formula-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Core Party Function</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Recruiting candidates</td><td>Identifying and supporting individuals to run for office under the party's label</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Mobilizing voters</td><td>Encouraging turnout and building coalitions of supporters</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Organizing government</td><td>Structuring legislative leadership, committee assignments, and voting coalitions</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Providing accountability</td><td>Giving voters a clear, simplified choice and a way to reward or punish a party's performance</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Three \"Faces\" of a Political Party</span>\n    <p>Political scientists commonly describe parties as operating in three related but distinct arenas: the <strong>party in the electorate</strong> (ordinary voters who identify with the party), the <strong>party organization</strong> (the formal structure — national, state, and local committees, staff, and officials), and the <strong>party in government</strong> (elected officials serving under the party's label, such as members of Congress). These three \"faces\" don't always act in perfect unison — a party's elected officials may sometimes diverge from either the party organization's official positions or its rank-and-file voters' preferences.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 3</span>\n    <p><strong>Identifying a Face of the Party.</strong> A state party's official committee endorses a specific candidate in a primary election. Identify which \"face\" of the party this action represents.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">This represents the <strong>party organization</strong> — the formal institutional structure of the party (in this case, a state committee) taking an official action, distinct from ordinary voters who identify with the party (the party in the electorate) or elected officials currently serving in government (the party in government).</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>List the core functions political parties perform as linkage institutions.</li>\n      <li>Distinguish the party in the electorate, the party organization, and the party in government.</li>\n      <li>Explain why these three \"faces\" of a party don't always act in perfect unison.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s54\"><span class=\"num\">5.4</span>Party Realignment and Change Over Time</h2>\n  <p>The two major parties' coalitions and relative strength have shifted meaningfully at various points in American history — a phenomenon political scientists study through the concepts of realignment and dealignment.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Realignment vs. Dealignment</span>\n    <p><strong>Realignment</strong> refers to a durable shift in which groups of voters support which party — often triggered by a \"critical election\" or period where a significant demographic or regional coalition shifts its party allegiance in a lasting way. <strong>Dealignment</strong>, by contrast, refers to a general weakening of attachment to either major party altogether — more voters identifying as independents, and existing partisans holding somewhat weaker or more flexible party loyalty than in earlier eras. These are two genuinely different patterns: realignment is about voters SWITCHING which party they support; dealignment is about voters becoming LESS attached to parties as a category in general.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>Divided government (different parties controlling the presidency and one or both chambers of Congress) and unified government (the same party controlling all three) are outcomes of specific ELECTIONS — they are not the same concept as realignment or dealignment, which describe longer-term shifts in voter coalitions and party attachment. A single election producing divided government doesn't necessarily indicate a realignment is underway; it may simply reflect that particular election's specific dynamics.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 4</span>\n    <p><strong>Distinguishing Realignment from Dealignment.</strong> Over several decades, a growing share of voters in a particular region stop identifying with either major party and increasingly register as independents, while still often voting for the same party in most elections. Is this best described as realignment or dealignment?</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">This is best described as <strong>dealignment</strong> — voters are weakening their FORMAL attachment to a party label (registering as independent) even while their underlying voting behavior remains fairly consistent. If instead this region's voters had durably SWITCHED which party they actually vote for and identify with, that would be realignment. The key distinguishing detail here is the weakening of party attachment itself, not a genuine switch in party support.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Distinguish party realignment from party dealignment.</li>\n      <li>Distinguish these long-term concepts from divided/unified government, which describe a single election's outcome.</li>\n      <li>Identify which pattern (realignment or dealignment) a described scenario illustrates.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s55\"><span class=\"num\">5.5</span>Third Parties and Their Role</h2>\n  <p>Third parties rarely win major elections in the American system — but understanding WHY requires looking at structural features of American elections themselves, not just assuming third parties simply lack popular support.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Winner-Take-All Elections Structurally Disadvantage Third Parties</span>\n    <p>Most American elections use a winner-take-all (plurality) system — whoever gets the most votes wins the entire seat or office, with no representation at all awarded to second- or third-place finishers. This structural feature (sometimes analyzed through what's called Duverger's Law) tends to produce and reinforce a two-party system over time, since voters and donors have a strong incentive to support one of the two parties most likely to actually win, rather than \"waste\" support on a third party unlikely to secure a plurality. Additional structural barriers — strict ballot access requirements, exclusion from major televised debates, and lack of eligibility for the same public matching funds as major parties — compound this disadvantage.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>Despite rarely winning, third parties are not politically irrelevant — they can meaningfully influence American politics in at least two ways: acting as a \"safety valve\" by raising issues the major parties initially ignore (issues major parties sometimes later adopt into their own platforms once a third party demonstrates real voter interest), and acting as a potential \"spoiler,\" drawing enough votes away from an ideologically similar major-party candidate to affect which major-party candidate ultimately wins. Don't describe third parties as having zero political impact just because they rarely win office outright.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 5</span>\n    <p><strong>Explaining Third-Party Disadvantage.</strong> Explain why a winner-take-all electoral system structurally disadvantages third parties, using a specific hypothetical example.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">Imagine three candidates competing for a single seat: a major-party candidate on each side, and a third-party candidate. If the third-party candidate receives 15% of the vote, they receive ZERO representation — all the seat's power goes entirely to whichever of the other two candidates receives the most votes, even if that's well under a majority. This all-or-nothing structure gives voters a strong incentive to support whichever major-party candidate they find more acceptable, rather than \"wasting\" their vote on the third-party option who has no realistic chance of actually winning the seat — reinforcing the two-party system over time, regardless of how much underlying support might exist for additional parties.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Explain how winner-take-all elections structurally disadvantage third parties.</li>\n      <li>List additional structural barriers (ballot access, debate exclusion, funding) third parties face.</li>\n      <li>Explain the \"safety valve\" and \"spoiler\" functions third parties can serve despite rarely winning office.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s56\"><span class=\"num\">5.6</span>Interest Groups and Policy Influence</h2>\n  <p>Interest groups are organizations of people who share a common goal and organize to influence public policy — a linkage institution distinct from political parties, since interest groups typically focus on specific issues rather than running candidates for office under their own banner.</p>\n\n  <table class=\"formula-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Interest Group Tactic</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Lobbying</td><td>Directly communicating with lawmakers or officials to influence legislation or regulation</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Litigation</td><td>Pursuing lawsuits or filing amicus curiae (\"friend of the court\") briefs to shape judicial outcomes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Grassroots mobilization</td><td>Organizing members and the public to contact officials, protest, or otherwise demonstrate support/opposition</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Campaign support</td><td>Contributing money or volunteer support to favored candidates, often through a PAC</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Iron Triangles Connect Agencies, Committees, and Interest Groups</span>\n    <p>An \"iron triangle\" describes a mutually reinforcing, ongoing relationship between a bureaucratic agency, a congressional committee (with jurisdiction over that agency), and an interest group with a direct stake in that agency's policy area. Each party benefits: the agency gains political support and expanded resources; the committee gains policy expertise and campaign support; the interest group gains favorable regulatory and legislative outcomes. This concept directly connects back to Unit 2's discussion of the federal bureaucracy, showing how policy influence often flows through durable, specialized relationships rather than through broad, general public pressure.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>Not all interest groups have equal resources or influence — well-funded groups with concentrated interests (like a specific industry) often organize more effectively than large, diffuse groups representing a broad public interest, even when the diffuse group's total membership is much larger. This connects directly back to the \"free rider problem\" from collective action theory: when a policy benefit would be broadly shared regardless of individual contribution, individuals have less incentive to personally contribute to the group's efforts, making it harder for large, diffuse-interest groups to organize as effectively as smaller, more concentrated-interest groups.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 6</span>\n    <p><strong>Explaining the Free Rider Problem.</strong> A proposed environmental regulation would benefit the general public broadly (cleaner air), but a specific industry would bear concentrated costs from compliance. Explain which side is likely to organize more effectively, and why.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">The <strong>industry</strong> (the concentrated-interest side) is likely to organize more effectively, despite representing far fewer people than \"the general public.\" Because the industry's costs are concentrated on a relatively small number of specific companies, each has a strong individual incentive to contribute to lobbying efforts against the regulation. The general public, by contrast, faces the <strong>free rider problem</strong> — since cleaner air would benefit everyone regardless of whether any specific individual personally contributes time or money to advocating for it, most individuals have comparatively weak incentive to organize, making the broad public interest harder to mobilize effectively despite its much larger size.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>List the major tactics interest groups use to influence policy.</li>\n      <li>Explain the iron triangle concept and how it connects agencies, committees, and interest groups.</li>\n      <li>Explain the free rider problem and why concentrated-interest groups often organize more effectively than diffuse-interest groups.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s57\"><span class=\"num\">5.7</span>Campaigns, Elections, and Campaign Finance</h2>\n  <p>This section covers the last of the course's 15 required Supreme Court cases — and it's one of the most frequently misunderstood, so precision matters especially here.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Citizens United v. FEC and Independent Expenditures</span>\n    <p><strong>Citizens United v. FEC (2010)</strong> ruled that government may not restrict INDEPENDENT political expenditures by corporations and unions, treating such spending as a form of protected political speech under the First Amendment. \"Independent expenditures\" specifically means spending that is NOT directly coordinated with a candidate's own campaign — for instance, an outside group running its own advertisement supporting or opposing a candidate, without coordinating that ad's content or strategy with the candidate's campaign itself.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — BCRA, Traditional PACs, and Super PACs</span>\n    <p>Before Citizens United, the <strong>Bipartisan Campaign Reform Act of 2002</strong> (BCRA, also called McCain-Feingold) banned \"soft money\" contributions to national party committees and restricted corporate/union-funded \"electioneering communications\" close to an election. Citizens United struck down BCRA's ban on independent expenditures by corporations and unions as an unconstitutional restriction on political speech — it did NOT touch BCRA's separate soft-money ban or the direct-contribution limit system, both of which remain in force.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <table class=\"formula-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Contribution Limits</th><th>May Give Directly to Candidates?</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Traditional/connected PAC</td><td>Subject to federal contribution limits</td><td>Yes, within those limits</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Super PAC</td><td>No limit on what it may raise or spend on independent expenditures</td><td>No — may not give directly to or coordinate with a candidate's campaign</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>This is one of the single most commonly misstated facts on the entire AP Gov exam: Citizens United did <strong>NOT</strong> eliminate limits on DIRECT contributions to candidates' own campaigns — those direct contribution limits remain in place. The ruling specifically concerns INDEPENDENT expenditures (spending not coordinated with a campaign), which is exactly why the case enabled the rise of \"Super PACs\" — political action committees that can raise and spend unlimited money on independent expenditures, but which are legally prohibited from directly coordinating that spending with a candidate's own campaign. Don't describe Citizens United as allowing unlimited direct contributions to candidates — that's a distinct, still-regulated category of campaign finance. (Note: the Super PAC structure itself was directly authorized by a related lower-court case, <em>SpeechNow.org v. FEC</em>, D.C. Cir. 2010, which applied Citizens United's reasoning to strike down contribution limits on independent-expenditure-only PACs — Citizens United made Super PACs possible, but SpeechNow.org is the case that specifically created the Super PAC structure.)</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 7</span>\n    <p><strong>Applying Citizens United.</strong> A corporation wants to run its own television advertisement supporting a candidate, without any input from or coordination with that candidate's campaign. Separately, the same corporation wants to donate directly to that candidate's official campaign fund beyond the legal contribution limit. Evaluate the legality of each action under Citizens United.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">The independent advertisement is <strong>protected</strong> under Citizens United — since it's not coordinated with the campaign, it qualifies as an independent expenditure, which the ruling protects as a form of free speech that government cannot limit. The direct donation beyond legal contribution limits, however, is <strong>still prohibited</strong> — Citizens United did not remove limits on direct contributions to a candidate's own campaign, which remain separately regulated. This distinction (independent expenditures vs. direct contributions) is exactly the nuance that's most frequently and easily misstated.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>State the precise holding of Citizens United v. FEC, specifically regarding independent expenditures.</li>\n      <li>Explain why the ruling led to the rise of Super PACs.</li>\n      <li>Distinguish independent expenditures (protected, unlimited under this ruling) from direct campaign contributions (still subject to separate legal limits).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s57b\">The Electoral College and Primaries</h2>\n  <p>Section 5.7's campaign finance material covers how campaigns are funded — but campaigns and elections also have a structure of their own, centered on one of this course's most heavily tested topics: how a U.S. president is actually elected.</p>\n\n  <table class=\"formula-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Total electors</td><td>538 (435 House + 100 Senate + 3 for D.C.)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Electoral votes needed to win</td><td>270 (a majority of 538)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Winner-Take-All Allocation and Swing-State Strategy</span>\n    <p>48 states and D.C. use <strong>winner-take-all</strong> allocation: whichever candidate wins the statewide popular vote receives ALL of that state's electoral votes. Maine and Nebraska instead use a <strong>congressional-district method</strong>: 2 electoral votes go to the statewide popular-vote winner, and 1 electoral vote goes to the popular-vote winner in each individual congressional district. Because winner-take-all makes campaigning in a \"safe\" (non-competitive) state yield no marginal electoral benefit — the state's electoral votes are already effectively decided — campaigns concentrate disproportionate time and money in a small number of competitive <strong>swing states</strong> (e.g., Pennsylvania, Wisconsin), where flipping the state can flip its entire electoral-vote bloc, rather than campaigning proportionally to the national popular vote.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"trap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label trap\">Watch Out For This</span>\n    <p>If no candidate reaches 270 electoral votes, the <strong>12th Amendment</strong> governs a contingency election: the House of Representatives chooses the President from the top three electoral-vote finishers, with each STATE DELEGATION casting a single vote (not one vote per representative) — meaning a small state's delegation carries the same weight as a large state's. The Senate separately chooses the Vice President from the top two finishers, with each senator casting an individual vote. Don't describe this as \"one vote per representative\" — the state-delegation rule is a specific, frequently tested detail.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 7b</span>\n    <p><strong>Applying Swing-State Strategy.</strong> Why does a presidential campaign spend disproportionate resources in Pennsylvania and Wisconsin rather than California or Texas, even though California and Texas have far more electoral votes?</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">California and Texas are considered \"safe\" states for one party under the current political landscape — because of winner-take-all allocation, additional campaigning there yields no marginal electoral benefit once a candidate is already expected to win (or lose) all of that state's electoral votes regardless. Pennsylvania and Wisconsin are competitive \"swing states\" where the statewide popular-vote winner is genuinely uncertain — meaning additional campaign resources there can plausibly flip the state's entire electoral-vote bloc from one candidate to the other, making the marginal value of campaigning in a swing state far higher than in a safe state, even one with more total electoral votes.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Primary Types</span>\n    <p>Before the general election, parties select their nominees through primary elections (or caucuses), which come in a few standard types: a <strong>closed primary</strong> allows only voters registered with that party to vote in its primary; an <strong>open primary</strong> allows any registered voter to vote in either party's primary, regardless of their own registration; a <strong>semi-closed (modified) primary</strong> allows registered party members plus unaffiliated/independent voters to participate, while still excluding voters registered with the opposing party.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 7c</span>\n    <p><strong>Applying Primary Types.</strong> A voter registered as an Independent lives in a state where only voters registered with a party may vote in that party's primary. Can this voter participate in either party's primary?</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">No — this state's rule describes a <strong>closed primary</strong>, which restricts participation to voters registered with that specific party. Since the voter is registered as an Independent rather than with either major party, they would not be eligible to vote in either party's closed primary under this rule. They would be able to participate if the state instead used an open or semi-closed primary system.</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>State the total number of electors (538) and the number needed to win (270).</li>\n      <li>Distinguish winner-take-all allocation (48 states + D.C.) from Maine/Nebraska's congressional-district method.</li>\n      <li>Explain the 12th Amendment's contingency election procedure, including the state-delegation voting rule in the House.</li>\n      <li>Explain why the Electoral College's structure produces a swing-state campaign strategy.</li>\n      <li>Distinguish closed, open, and semi-closed primaries.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"s58\"><span class=\"num\">5.8</span>The Media's Role in Politics</h2>\n  <p>The media functions as a linkage institution in its own right — shaping not just what citizens know about politics, but which issues seem important enough to pay attention to in the first place.</p>\n\n  <table class=\"formula-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Media Function/Effect</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Watchdog function</td><td>Investigating and exposing government wrongdoing or misconduct</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Agenda-setting</td><td>Shaping which issues the public perceives as important, based on what receives coverage</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Horse-race journalism</td><td>Coverage emphasizing who is \"winning\" a campaign (polling, strategy) over substantive policy discussion</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Selective exposure</td><td>Individuals seeking out media sources that reinforce their existing views, potentially deepening polarization</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"key-idea\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label idea\">Key Idea — Agenda-Setting Is About WHAT to Think ABOUT, Not WHAT to Think</span>\n    <p>The agenda-setting effect specifically describes media's power to influence which issues the public considers important and worth paying attention to — it is a subtly different claim from saying media directly tells people WHAT opinion to hold on a given issue. A news outlet that runs extensive coverage of immigration policy, for instance, may not succeed in changing anyone's specific opinion on the issue, but by covering it heavily, it likely does succeed in making the public perceive immigration policy as a more pressing, salient national concern than it otherwise might have — that's the specific mechanism agenda-setting describes.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"worked\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label example\">Worked Example 8</span>\n    <p><strong>Identifying Horse-Race Journalism.</strong> A news broadcast covering an upcoming election spends most of its airtime discussing which candidate is ahead in the polls and their campaign strategy, with comparatively little discussion of the candidates' actual policy positions. Identify this pattern and explain a potential consequence.</p>\n    <details class=\"solution\"><summary>Show the reasoning</summary>\n      <div class=\"sol-content\">This is <strong>horse-race journalism</strong> — media coverage emphasizing the competitive, \"who's winning\" dimension of a campaign over substantive policy discussion. A potential consequence is that voters may end up with a well-developed sense of which candidate is currently favored to win, while remaining comparatively uninformed about the actual policy differences between the candidates — potentially making it harder for voters to cast a genuinely informed, policy-based vote (which connects back to the rational choice voting model from Section 5.1).</div>\n    </details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"recap\">\n    <span class=\"tag-label recap\">Before You Move On</span>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Distinguish the media's watchdog function, agenda-setting effect, and tendency toward horse-race journalism.</li>\n      <li>Explain the precise distinction between agenda-setting (what to think ABOUT) and directly shaping opinion (what to think).</li>\n      <li>Explain selective exposure and its potential connection to political polarization.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"practice\">Unit 5 Practice Set</h2>\n  <p>This is the final practice set of the entire course — work through every problem carefully before checking the answer key.</p>\n\n  <h3>Conceptual</h3>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">1</span><span>Explain the difference between having a legal right to vote and actually exercising that right, using an example.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">2</span><span>Distinguish the party in the electorate, the party organization, and the party in government.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">3</span><span>Explain how a winner-take-all electoral system structurally disadvantages third parties.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">4</span><span>Explain the free rider problem and its effect on interest group organization.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">5</span><span>State the precise holding of Citizens United v. FEC, including what it does NOT permit.</span></div>\n\n  <h3>Application</h3>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">6</span><span>A voter chooses a candidate specifically because that candidate's tax policy proposal matches their own preferences. Which voting model does this illustrate?</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">7</span><span>A state significantly simplifies its voter registration process. Predict the likely effect on turnout, and explain why.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">8</span><span>Over several election cycles, a major regional voting bloc durably shifts its support from one major party to the other. Is this best described as realignment or dealignment?</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">9</span><span>A regulatory agency, a congressional committee, and an industry association consistently work together on policy affecting that industry. Identify this relationship.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">10</span><span>A corporation runs its own independent television ad supporting a candidate, without coordinating with the candidate's campaign. Evaluate the legality of this action.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">11</span><span>A news outlet devotes most of its campaign coverage to polling data and strategy rather than policy substance. Identify this pattern.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">12</span><span>A third-party candidate receives 12% of the vote in a winner-take-all election but wins no seats. Explain why, and identify one way this candidate's campaign might still influence policy.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"practice-item\"><span class=\"qnum-badge\">13</span><span>Explain the distinction between the media's agenda-setting effect and directly shaping public opinion on an issue.</span></div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"margin-top:30px;\">Answer Key</h3>\n  <div class=\"answer-key\">\n    <details><summary>Question 1</summary><div class=\"a-content\">A citizen may be legally entitled to vote (e.g., a registered adult citizen) but choose not to, due to factors like low interest, administrative barriers, or a race not feeling competitive — the legal right and the actual behavior of turning out are related but distinct.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 2</summary><div class=\"a-content\">The party in the electorate is ordinary voters who identify with the party. The party organization is the formal institutional structure (committees, staff). The party in government is elected officials serving under the party's label.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 3</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Since the winner receives all the representation and losing candidates receive none, voters have a strong incentive to support one of the two candidates most likely to actually win, rather than \"waste\" their vote on a third-party candidate unlikely to secure a plurality.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 4</summary><div class=\"a-content\">When a policy benefit would be broadly shared regardless of individual contribution, individuals have less incentive to personally contribute to the group's organizing efforts, making large, diffuse-interest groups harder to organize effectively than smaller, concentrated-interest groups.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 5</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Citizens United ruled that government cannot restrict independent political expenditures by corporations and unions, treating them as protected speech. It did NOT remove limits on direct contributions to a candidate's own campaign, which remain separately regulated.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 6</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Rational choice voting — the voter is selecting the candidate whose specific policy position most closely matches their own preference.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 7</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Turnout would likely increase, since simplifying registration removes a structural/administrative barrier that otherwise prevents some eligible citizens from voting, even when they are legally entitled to.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 8</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Realignment — a durable shift in which party a specific voting bloc supports, as opposed to dealignment, which would describe weakening attachment to parties generally rather than switching allegiance.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 9</summary><div class=\"a-content\">An iron triangle — a mutually reinforcing relationship between a bureaucratic agency, a congressional committee, and an interest group with a shared stake in that policy area.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 10</summary><div class=\"a-content\">This is legal under Citizens United — an independent expenditure not coordinated with the campaign is protected political speech that the government cannot restrict.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 11</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Horse-race journalism — media coverage emphasizing the competitive, \"who's winning\" dimension of a campaign over substantive policy discussion.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 12</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Because the winner-take-all system awards all representation to the plurality winner, with none for the third-place finisher, regardless of their vote share. Despite winning no seats, the campaign could still influence policy by raising an issue the major parties later adopt, or by acting as a \"spoiler\" affecting which major-party candidate ultimately won.</div></details>\n    <details><summary>Question 13</summary><div class=\"a-content\">Agenda-setting shapes which issues the public perceives as important and worth paying attention to — it does not necessarily change what specific opinion the public holds on that issue, a subtly different and more direct form of influence.</div></details>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"footer-nav\">\n    <strong>That completes all 5 units of AP U.S. Government and Politics.</strong> With every unit built, the highest-value use of remaining study time is rotating back through Units 2 and 5 — the two largest exam categories — while keeping the 9 foundational documents and 15 required Supreme Court cases fresh through regular, spaced review.\n  </div>\n\n</div>\n"
};

